<article class="ltx_document ltx_authors_1line">
<h1 class="ltx_title ltx_title_document" style="font-size:207%;">Diffusion<sup id="id1" class="ltx_sup">2</sup>: Dual Diffusion Model with Uncertainty-Aware Adaptive Noise for Momentary Trajectory Prediction</h1>
<div class="ltx_authors">
<span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Yuhao Luo<sup id="id2" class="ltx_sup">†</sup>
</span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Yuang Zhang<sup id="id3" class="ltx_sup">†</sup>
</span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Kehua Chen
</span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Xinyu Zheng
</span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Shucheng Zhang
</span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Sikai Chen
</span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Yinhai Wang
</span><span id="id4" class="ltx_note ltx_note_frontmatter ltx_thanks_contribution ltx_role_thanks"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">†</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">†</sup><span class="ltx_note_type">thanks: </span><sup id="id4.1" class="ltx_sup">†</sup> Yuhao Luo and Yuang Zhang contributed equally to this work.</span></span></span><span id="id5" class="ltx_note ltx_note_frontmatter ltx_thanks_note ltx_role_thanks"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">†</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">†</sup><span class="ltx_note_type">thanks: </span>Yuhao Luo and Sikai Chen are with Civil and Environmental Engineering Department, University of Wisconsin-Madison, Madison, USA</span></span></span><span id="id6" class="ltx_note ltx_note_frontmatter ltx_thanks_note ltx_role_thanks"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">†</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">†</sup><span class="ltx_note_type">thanks: </span>Yuang Zhang, Kehua Chen, Shucheng Zhang and Yinhai Wang are with Civil and Environmental Engineering Department, University of Washington, Seattle, USA</span></span></span><span id="id7" class="ltx_note ltx_note_frontmatter ltx_thanks_note ltx_role_thanks"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">†</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">†</sup><span class="ltx_note_type">thanks: </span>Xinyu Zheng is with the College of Transportation, Tongji University, Shanghai, China</span></span></span><span id="id8" class="ltx_note ltx_note_frontmatter ltx_thanks_correspondence ltx_role_thanks"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">†</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">†</sup><span class="ltx_note_type">thanks: </span>* Kehua Chen and Yinhai Wang are the corresponding authors, E-mail: zeonchen@uw.edu; yinhai@uw.edu.</span></span></span></span></div>

<div id="abstract1" class="ltx_abstract"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_abstract">Abstract</h6>
    
<p id="abstract1.1" class="ltx_p">Accurate pedestrian trajectory prediction is crucial for ensuring safety and efficiency in autonomous driving and human-robot interaction scenarios. Earlier studies primarily utilized sufficient observational data to predict future trajectories. However, in real-world scenarios, such as pedestrians suddenly emerging from blind spots, sufficient observational data is often unavailable (i.e. momentary trajectory), making accurate prediction challenging and increasing the risk of traffic accidents. Therefore, advancing research on pedestrian trajectory prediction under extreme scenarios is critical for enhancing traffic safety. In this work, we propose a novel framework termed <span id="abstract1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Diffusion<sup id="abstract1.1.1.1" class="ltx_sup">2</sup></span>, tailored for momentary trajectory prediction. <span id="abstract1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Diffusion<sup id="abstract1.1.2.1" class="ltx_sup">2</sup></span> consists of two sequentially connected diffusion models: one for <span id="abstract1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">backward prediction</span>, which generates unobserved historical trajectories, and the other for <span id="abstract1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">forward prediction</span>, which forecasts future trajectories. Given that the generated unobserved historical trajectories may introduce additional noise, we propose a dual-head parameterization mechanism to estimate their aleatoric uncertainty and design a temporally adaptive noise module that dynamically modulates the noise scale in the forward diffusion process. Empirically, <span id="abstract1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Diffusion<sup id="abstract1.1.5.1" class="ltx_sup">2</sup></span> sets a new state-of-the-art in momentary trajectory prediction on ETH/UCY and Stanford Drone datasets.</p>
  
</div>
<div class="ltx_keywords">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_keywords">Index Terms: </h6>Momentary trajectory prediction, uncertainty-aware, diffusion model, aleatoric uncertainty

</div>
<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">I </span><span id="S1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Introduction</span></h2>

<div id="S1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Accurate pedestrian trajectory prediction is essential for autonomous driving, as it enhances vehicle safety—particularly in scenarios involving human-vehicle interactions <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">1</a>, <a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">2</a>]</cite>. In recent years, a wide variety of approaches have been developed to tackle this task. Typically, these approaches focus on forecasting future trajectories based on a sufficiently long observation period, such as 8 frames (about 3.2 seconds) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">3</a>, <a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>, <a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>]</cite>.
However, in many real-world scenarios, such as when a pedestrian suddenly appears behind an obstacle (e.g., a building or a truck), the vehicle lacks sufficient time to gather adequate observational data. This poses significant challenges to conventional prediction methods, often leading to a substantial degradation in prediction performance <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>]</cite> and even increasing the risk of traffic accidents.
As reported in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>]</cite>, the occurrence frequency of pedestrians with only momentary observations reaches <math id="S1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2.22\ s^{-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2.22</mn><mo lspace="0.500em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>s</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2.22\ s^{-1}</annotation></semantics></math> in SDD <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">7</a>]</cite> and <math id="S1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1.02\ s^{-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1.02</mn><mo lspace="0.500em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>s</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1.02\ s^{-1}</annotation></semantics></math> in ETH/UCY <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">8</a>]</cite> datasets. Therefore, it is crucial to investigate pedestrian trajectory prediction using limited observational data.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">To address the aforementioned challenges, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>]</cite> proposed <span id="S1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">momentary trajectory prediction</span> using only two observed frames. They introduced a Momentary Observation feature Extractor (MOE) that integrates social and scene context at the data level and improves representation learning via soft pre-training with masked trajectory and context restoration tasks.
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">9</a>]</cite> proposed a teacher–student framework where the teacher, trained on rich data, guides a student with limited inputs. However, using only two frames restricts temporal understanding. To bridge this gap, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">10</a>]</cite> deployed a bidirectional consistent diffusion framework (BCDiff) to jointly generate both unobserved historical trajectories and future trajectories.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">While <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">10</a>]</cite> has demonstrated the effectiveness of jointly predicting historical trajectories to enrich contextual information and compensate for the limitations of momentary observations, it involved simultaneously co-predicting historical and future trajectories, overlooking the causal relationship between the two components. In this study, we predict historical and future trajectories separately in a sequential manner. Specifically, we propose a model named <span id="S1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Diffusion<sup id="S1.p3.1.1.1" class="ltx_sup">2</sup></span> for momentary pedestrian trajectory prediction.
Building on the strengths of diffusion models, our framework employs two sequentially connected components: one for <span id="S1.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">backward prediction</span> and the other for <span id="S1.p3.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">forward prediction</span>, as illustrated in Fig. <a href="#S1.F1" title="Fig. 1 ‣ I Introduction ‣ Diffusion2: Dual Diffusion Model with Uncertainty-Aware Adaptive Noise for Momentary Trajectory Prediction" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>.
A follow-up concern is the reliability of the predicted historical trajectories: if they are highly inaccurate or noisy, they may adversely affect the subsequent prediction of future trajectories. Therefore, it is essential to quantify the uncertainty of the predicted historical trajectories and selectively utilize reliable information for downstream prediction tasks. However, diffusion models only implicitly learn data distributions and lack the ability to explicitly quantify uncertainty. To fill this gap, we design a dual-head parameterization mechanism that augments a standard noise prediction network by introducing two output heads. The first head predicts the noise, while the second estimates the log-variance for each coordinate, thereby enabling direct quantification of aleatoric uncertainty.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Afterwards, to incorporate the estimated aleatoric uncertainties, we design a temporally adaptive noise scheduler that dynamically injects noise into the forward diffusion model based on the predicted uncertainty levels. Intuitively, when historical trajectories are highly uncertain, more noise is injected into the forward diffusion model to encourage exploration during generation, and less noise is added when uncertainty is low.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">To sum up, the main contributions of our study are:</p>
<ul id="S1.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S1.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We propose <span id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Diffusion<sup id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1.1.1" class="ltx_sup">2</sup></span>, a novel framework consisting of two sequential diffusion models that capture the causal dependencies of trajectories: one for <span id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">backward prediction</span> and the other for <span id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">forward prediction</span>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We propose a dual-head parameterization mechanism that enables the diffusion model for <span id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">backward prediction</span> to quantify aleatoric uncertainty in a single sampling pass, and further introduce an adaptive noise scheduling strategy for the <span id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">forward prediction</span> diffusion model, which dynamically adjusts the noise magnitude based on the estimated uncertainty.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Our model achieves state-of-the-art accuracy in momentary pedestrian prediction, as demonstrated on the ETH/UCY and Stanford Drone datasets.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
<figure id="S1.F1" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2510.04365v1/DiffFrame.svg" id="S1.F1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:405/133;" width="405" height="133"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Fig. 1: </span>Three distinct frameworks are proposed to address momentary trajectory prediction. (a) The model directly utilizes observable trajectories to predict future trajectories. (b) The approach jointly predicts unobservable historical trajectories and future trajectories. (c) Our proposed framework, <span id="S1.F1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Diffusion<sup id="S1.F1.4.1" class="ltx_sup">2</sup></span>, consists of two sequentially connected diffusion models: one dedicated to <span id="S1.F1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">backward prediction</span>, and the other to <span id="S1.F1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">forward prediction</span>.</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">II </span><span id="S2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Related Works</span></h2>

<section id="S2.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S2.SS1.4" class="ltx_text">II-A</span> </span><span id="S2.SS1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Traditional Trajectory Prediction</span></h3>

<div id="S2.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Traditional human trajectory prediction tasks focus on forecasting future movements based on sufficiently long historical observations. To enhance the prediction accuracy, a variety of methods have been proposed to capture the temporal features and spatial features, with a particular focus on inter-pedestrian interactions and pedestrian-environment relationships. To capture the temporal features, researchers typically employed Long-Short Term Memory (LSTM) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">11</a>, <a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">12</a>, <a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">13</a>, <a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">14</a>, <a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">15</a>]</cite> and Recurrent Neural Networks (RNNs) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">16</a>, <a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">17</a>, <a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">18</a>]</cite> to model the temporal dynamics of pedestrian states. For modeling spatial interactions, social pooling mechanism <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">19</a>, <a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">20</a>]</cite>, graph-based modeling <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">21</a>, <a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">22</a>, <a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">23</a>, <a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">24</a>]</cite> and attention mechanism <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">25</a>, <a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">26</a>, <a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">27</a>, <a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">28</a>]</cite> are commonly employed to represent inter-agent and agent-environment dynamics. In addition, many physics-based models <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">29</a>]</cite>, such as Social Force Model (SFM) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">30</a>, <a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">31</a>, <a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">32</a>]</cite> and Energy-based Models <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">33</a>, <a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">34</a>]</cite>, have been utilized to provide more interactive information. However, the aforementioned methods assume ideal conditions with ample historical observations, and therefore may not generalize well to extreme cases <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>]</cite>. In contrast, this work focuses on trajectory prediction under extreme scenarios, aiming to accurately forecast future trajectories given only two observed frames.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S2.SS2.4" class="ltx_text">II-B</span> </span><span id="S2.SS2.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Momentary Trajectory Prediction</span></h3>

<div id="S2.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Momentary trajectory prediction aims to explore the limits of forecasting accuracy under extreme conditions, where only two historical frames are provided as input. In <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>]</cite>, the authors proposed a novel fusion mechanism that integrates social and scene context information at the data level, and then pre-trained the Momentary Observation Feature Extraction (MOE) module on several sub-tasks, such as masked trajectory completion and context restoration, to learn joint representations. To address the lack of temporal information under limited observations, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">9</a>]</cite> adopted a teacher-student framework, where the teacher network, trained on rich 8-frame sequences, distills its spatial-temporal reasoning ability into a student network that learns to make accurate predictions using only 2 frames. As an alternative approach, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">10</a>]</cite> proposed a Bi-directional Consistent Diffusion framework (BCDiff) that jointly infers unobserved historical and future trajectories in a step-by-step manner to capture the bidirectional dependencies between them.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S2.SS3.4" class="ltx_text">II-C</span> </span><span id="S2.SS3.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Diffusion Models</span></h3>

<div id="S2.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Diffusion models have achieved remarkable success in a variety of challenging domains <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib35" title="" class="ltx_ref">35</a>, <a href="#bib.bib36" title="" class="ltx_ref">36</a>, <a href="#bib.bib37" title="" class="ltx_ref">37</a>]</cite>, such as image synthesis <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib38" title="" class="ltx_ref">38</a>, <a href="#bib.bib39" title="" class="ltx_ref">39</a>, <a href="#bib.bib40" title="" class="ltx_ref">40</a>]</cite>, computer vision <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib41" title="" class="ltx_ref">41</a>, <a href="#bib.bib42" title="" class="ltx_ref">42</a>, <a href="#bib.bib43" title="" class="ltx_ref">43</a>]</cite>, and natural language processing <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib44" title="" class="ltx_ref">44</a>, <a href="#bib.bib45" title="" class="ltx_ref">45</a>]</cite>, demonstrating their versatility and robustness. Recently, diffusion models have been deployed to trajectory prediction task. MID <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib46" title="" class="ltx_ref">46</a>]</cite> designed a transformer-based network architecture to model the complex temporal dependencies in trajectories. EquiDiff <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib47" title="" class="ltx_ref">47</a>]</cite> integrated diffusion modeling with equivariant transformers to ensure equivariant properties throughout the prediction process. LED <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib48" title="" class="ltx_ref">48</a>]</cite> employed a trainable leapfrog initializer in the trajectory prediction task to skip a large number of denoising steps, thereby significantly reducing inference time. In our work, we also leverage a diffusion model to achieve momentary trajectory prediction.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S2.SS4.4" class="ltx_text">II-D</span> </span><span id="S2.SS4.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Aleatoric Uncertainty</span></h3>

<div id="S2.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Aleatoric uncertainty (AU) captures the irreducible, data-driven randomness present even when a model is perfectly specified. In trajectory prediction tasks, AU originates from sensor noise, measurement errors and the inherently stochastic nature of human or agent behaviour <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib49" title="" class="ltx_ref">49</a>]</cite>. Since this variability is tied to the data-collection process itself, AU cannot be reduced by collecting additional training data. The approaches to quantifying AU fall into two categories: explicit likelihood models and implicit likelihood models. Explicit likelihood models (e.g. heteroscedastic Gaussian <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib50" title="" class="ltx_ref">50</a>]</cite> and neural processes <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib51" title="" class="ltx_ref">51</a>]</cite>) output the full predictive distribution parameters directly; AU is then available analytically as the predictive variance. Implicit likelihood models (e.g. diffusion models <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib38" title="" class="ltx_ref">38</a>]</cite>, VAE <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib52" title="" class="ltx_ref">52</a>]</cite>) cannot provide a closed-form predictive variance, so AU must be approximated via Monte Carlo sampling. In this work, we propose a diffusion-based aleatoric uncertainty estimation method to quantify AU.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">III </span><span id="S3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Preliminaries</span></h2>

<section id="S3.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S3.SS1.4" class="ltx_text">III-A</span> </span><span id="S3.SS1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Problem Definition</span></h3>

<div id="S3.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this study, we aim to perform accurate momentary pedestrian trajectory prediction using only two observed frames as input. These observed frames are defined as
<math id="S3.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathbf{x}^{obs}=\left\{x_{t},\mathbf{p}_{t},\mathbf{S}\mid t=-1,0\right\}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>𝐱</mi><mrow><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐩</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐒</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><mi>t</mi><mo rspace="0em">=</mo><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>}</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x}^{obs}=\left\{x_{t},\mathbf{p}_{t},\mathbf{S}\mid t=-1,0\right\},</annotation></semantics></math>
where <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the 2D position of the ego pedestrian, <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{p}_{t}\in\mathbb{R}^{N\times 2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐩</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{p}_{t}\in\mathbb{R}^{N\times 2}</annotation></semantics></math> represents the positions of surrounding pedestrians, with <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> denoting the number of surrounding pedestrians, and <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐒</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{S}</annotation></semantics></math> corresponds to the semantic scene map. The goal is to predict future trajectories
<math id="S3.SS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{y}=\left\{x_{t}\right\}_{t=1}^{T_{fut}}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝐲</mi><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo>{</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub></msubsup></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{y}=\left\{x_{t}\right\}_{t=1}^{T_{fut}},</annotation></semantics></math>
with <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{fut}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{fut}</annotation></semantics></math> indicating the prediction horizon. In addition, we define the unobserved historical trajectories as
<math id="S3.SS1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{x}^{ubs}=\left\{x_{t}\right\}_{t=-1-T_{ubs}}^{-2}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>𝐱</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow></msup><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo>{</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msubsup></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x}^{ubs}=\left\{x_{t}\right\}_{t=-1-T_{ubs}}^{-2},</annotation></semantics></math>
where <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{ubs}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{ubs}</annotation></semantics></math> is the length of the unobserved historical. Our model predicts future trajectories by leveraging inferred historical trajectories based on only a few observed points. Since the inferred historical trajectories may be noisy, our model also accounts for their uncertainty.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S3.SS2.4" class="ltx_text">III-B</span> </span><span id="S3.SS2.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Denoising Diffusion Probabilistic Model</span></h3>

<div id="S3.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The Denoising Diffusion Probabilistic Model (DDPM) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib38" title="" class="ltx_ref">38</a>]</cite> typically consists of two Markov chain processes: a forward diffusion process and a reverse denoising process. To avoid redundancy, we present the diffusion formulation for <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐲</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{y}</annotation></semantics></math> only and note that the same process can be directly applied to <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{x}^{ubs}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝐱</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x}^{ubs}</annotation></semantics></math>. The forward diffusion process gradually corrupts the ground-truth data by adding Gaussian noise over multiple time steps, forming a sequence of perturbed samples <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\left\{\mathbf{y}_{0},\mathbf{y}_{1},\ldots,\mathbf{y}_{M}\right\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>{</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐲</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐲</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐲</mi><mi>M</mi></msub></mrow><mo>}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left\{\mathbf{y}_{0},\mathbf{y}_{1},\ldots,\mathbf{y}_{M}\right\}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{y}_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐲</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{y}_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> is the original data and <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> is the number of diffusion steps. Generally, we define the diffusion process as:</p>
<table id="S3.EGx1" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.E1"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E1.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle q(\mathbf{y}_{1:M}\mid\mathbf{y}_{0})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐲</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><msub><mi>𝐲</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle q(\mathbf{y}_{1:M}\mid\mathbf{y}_{0})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle:=\prod_{m=1}^{M}q(\mathbf{y}_{m}\mid\mathbf{y}_{n})," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∏</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>M</mi></munderover></mstyle><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐲</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><msub><mi>𝐲</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle:=\prod_{m=1}^{M}q(\mathbf{y}_{m}\mid\mathbf{y}_{n}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(1)</span></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S3.E2"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle q(\mathbf{y}_{m}\mid\mathbf{y}_{0})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐲</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><msub><mi>𝐲</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle q(\mathbf{y}_{m}\mid\mathbf{y}_{0})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle:=\mathcal{N}\!\big(\mathbf{y}_{m};\alpha_{m}\mathbf{y}_{0},\;\sigma_{m}^{2}\,\bm{I}\big)." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mpadded style="width:0.798em;" width="0.798em"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi></mpadded><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐲</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝐲</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mi>m</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝑰</mi></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle:=\mathcal{N}\!\big(\mathbf{y}_{m};\alpha_{m}\mathbf{y}_{0},\;\sigma_{m}^{2}\,\bm{I}\big).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(2)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q(\mathbf{y}_{M})\sim\mathcal{N}(\mathbf{0},\bm{I})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐲</mi><mi>M</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>𝟎</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>𝑰</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q(\mathbf{y}_{M})\sim\mathcal{N}(\mathbf{0},\bm{I})</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=m-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=m-1</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{m}</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the standard deviation (so the covariance is <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{m}^{2}\bm{I})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo fence="true" rspace="0em">OPEN</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mi>m</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝑰</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{m}^{2}\bm{I})</annotation></semantics></math>. The diffusion parameters <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha_{m},\ \sigma_{m}&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.667em">,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha_{m},\ \sigma_{m}&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> induce a noise schedule over the time steps such that the Signal-to-Noise Ratio (SNR) <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nu(m)=\alpha_{m}^{2}/\sigma_{m}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>ν</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>α</mi><mi>m</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo>/</mo><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mi>m</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nu(m)=\alpha_{m}^{2}/\sigma_{m}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> is strictly monotonically decreasing in <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Conversely, the reverse denoising process is trained to iteratively remove noise in a step-by-step fashion along this Markov chain, reconstructing the original trajectory conditioned on context information. Generally, we define the reverse denoising process as:</p>
<table id="S3.EGx2" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.E3"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E3.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle p_{\theta}(\mathbf{y}_{0:M})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐲</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle p_{\theta}(\mathbf{y}_{0:M})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle:=p(\mathbf{y}_{M})\prod_{m=1}^{M}p_{\theta}(\mathbf{y}_{n}\mid\mathbf{y}_{m})," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐲</mi><mi>M</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∏</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>M</mi></munderover></mstyle><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐲</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><msub><mi>𝐲</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle:=p(\mathbf{y}_{M})\prod_{m=1}^{M}p_{\theta}(\mathbf{y}_{n}\mid\mathbf{y}_{m}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(3)</span></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S3.E4"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle p_{\theta}(\mathbf{y}_{n}\mid\mathbf{y}_{m})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐲</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><msub><mi>𝐲</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle p_{\theta}(\mathbf{y}_{n}\mid\mathbf{y}_{m})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle:=\mathcal{N}\!\big(\bm{\mu}_{p},\,\bm{\Sigma}_{p}\big)," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mpadded style="width:0.798em;" width="0.798em"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi></mpadded><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><msub><mi>𝝁</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝚺</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle:=\mathcal{N}\!\big(\bm{\mu}_{p},\,\bm{\Sigma}_{p}\big),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(4)</span></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S3.E5"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\bm{\mu}_{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝝁</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\bm{\mu}_{p}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\frac{\alpha_{m\mid n}\,\sigma_{n}^{2}}{\sigma_{m}^{2}}\,\mathbf{y}_{m}+\frac{\sigma_{m\mid n}^{2}\,\alpha_{n}}{\sigma_{m}^{2}}\,\mathbf{y}_{\theta}(\mathbf{y}_{m},m)," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo fence="false">|</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mi>m</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝐲</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo fence="false">|</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mi>m</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝐲</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐲</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\frac{\alpha_{m\mid n}\,\sigma_{n}^{2}}{\sigma_{m}^{2}}\,\mathbf{y}_{m}+\frac{\sigma_{m\mid n}^{2}\,\alpha_{n}}{\sigma_{m}^{2}}\,\mathbf{y}_{\theta}(\mathbf{y}_{m},m),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(5)</span></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S3.E6"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\bm{\Sigma}_{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝚺</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\bm{\Sigma}_{p}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\frac{\sigma_{n}^{2}\,\sigma_{m\mid n}^{2}}{\sigma_{m}^{2}}\,\bm{I}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo fence="false">|</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mi>m</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝑰</mi></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\frac{\sigma_{n}^{2}\,\sigma_{m\mid n}^{2}}{\sigma_{m}^{2}}\,\bm{I}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(6)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS2.p2.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha_{m\mid n}=\frac{\alpha_{m}}{\alpha_{n}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo fence="false">|</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mfrac><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha_{m\mid n}=\frac{\alpha_{m}}{\alpha_{n}}</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma^{2}_{m\mid n}=\sigma_{m}^{2}-{\alpha^{2}_{m\mid n}}{\sigma_{n}^{2}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo fence="false">|</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mi>m</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo>−</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>α</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo fence="false">|</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma^{2}_{m\mid n}=\sigma_{m}^{2}-{\alpha^{2}_{m\mid n}}{\sigma_{n}^{2}}</annotation></semantics></math>; <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{y}_{\theta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐲</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{y}_{\theta}</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the neural network with parameters <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>θ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta</annotation></semantics></math> to approximate <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{y}_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐲</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{y}_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p(\mathbf{y}_{M})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐲</mi><mi>M</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p(\mathbf{y}_{M})</annotation></semantics></math> is the standard Gaussian noise used as the starting point. In addition, <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\mu}_{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝝁</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\mu}_{p}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\Sigma}_{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝚺</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\Sigma}_{p}</annotation></semantics></math> denote the predicted mean and variance, respectively.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">IV </span><span id="S4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Methods</span></h2>

<section id="S4.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S4.SS1.4" class="ltx_text">IV-A</span> </span><span id="S4.SS1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Overall Architecture</span></h3>

<div id="S4.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Our proposed model, <span id="S4.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Diffusion<sup id="S4.SS1.p1.1.1.1" class="ltx_sup">2</sup></span>, is a two-stage diffusion framework consisting of two sequentially connected modules: <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textit{DDPM}_{past}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">DDPM</mtext><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textit{DDPM}_{past}</annotation></semantics></math>, which performs backward trajectory prediction and estimates aleatoric uncertainty, and <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textit{DDPM}_{fut}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">DDPM</mtext><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textit{DDPM}_{fut}</annotation></semantics></math>, which predicts future trajectories, as illustrated in Fig. <a href="#S4.F2" title="Fig. 2 ‣ IV-A Overall Architecture ‣ IV Methods ‣ Diffusion2: Dual Diffusion Model with Uncertainty-Aware Adaptive Noise for Momentary Trajectory Prediction" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>. Given the observed frames <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{x}^{obs}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝐱</mi><mrow><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x}^{obs}</annotation></semantics></math>, a shared encoder first extracts a contextual representation <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{h}_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐡</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{h}_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>. Conditioned on <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{h}_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐡</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{h}_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textit{DDPM}_{past}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">DDPM</mtext><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textit{DDPM}_{past}</annotation></semantics></math> predicts the unobserved historical trajectory <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{x}^{ubs}_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>𝐱</mi><mn>0</mn><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x}^{ubs}_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> and simultaneously estimates its aleatoric uncertainty <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{u}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐮</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{u}</annotation></semantics></math> through a dual-head parameterization mechanism. Sequentially, we employ a trajectory encoder consisting of two LSTM layers followed by a three-layers MLP to extract the features <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{v}_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐯</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{v}_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> from <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{x}^{ubs}_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>𝐱</mi><mn>0</mn><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x}^{ubs}_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>. The extracted feature <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{v}_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐯</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{v}_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> is then fused with the contextual representation <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{h}_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐡</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{h}_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> to form a new conditioning vector <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{h}_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐡</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{h}_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>, which serves as the input context for <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textit{DDPM}_{fut}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">DDPM</mtext><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textit{DDPM}_{fut}</annotation></semantics></math> to predict future trajectories <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{y}_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐲</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{y}_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Furthermore, to incorporate the estimated aleatoric uncertainties, we introduce a learned temporally adaptive noise scheduling strategy for <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textit{DDPM}_{fut}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">DDPM</mtext><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textit{DDPM}_{fut}</annotation></semantics></math>, where a gamma module dynamically adjusts the noise scale based on <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{u}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐮</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{u}</annotation></semantics></math> and the current diffusion timestep <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math>. Note that designing a sophisticated network to extract spatio-temporal features from trajectories is not the main focus of this work, and <span id="S4.SS1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Diffusion<sup id="S4.SS1.p1.1.2.1" class="ltx_sup">2</sup></span> is an encoder-agnostic framework that can seamlessly plug into the diverse encoders proposed in prior studies. In our experiments, we adopt the MOE <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>]</cite> encoder owing to its superior representational capacity. As the denoising backbone for both <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textit{DDPM}_{past}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">DDPM</mtext><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textit{DDPM}_{past}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textit{DDPM}_{fut}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">DDPM</mtext><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textit{DDPM}_{fut}</annotation></semantics></math>, we adopt a streamlined Transformer decoder <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib46" title="" class="ltx_ref">46</a>]</cite>. Parallel fully connected layers first project the corrupted trajectory sample and its contextual features into a common latent space, after which we add a sinusoidal encoding of the current diffusion timestep. This sequence is then processed by three stacked self-attention blocks that capture spatio-temporal dependencies. A final linear layer subsequently maps the representation back to 2-D coordinate space.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.F2" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2510.04365v1/framework.png" id="S4.F2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/169;" width="476" height="169" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Fig. 2: </span>The overview of our proposed <span id="S4.F2.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Diffusion<sup id="S4.F2.6.1" class="ltx_sup">2</sup></span>:
(a) <span id="S4.F2.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Framework</span>: <span id="S4.F2.8" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Diffusion<sup id="S4.F2.8.1" class="ltx_sup">2</sup></span> consists of two sequentially connected diffusion models, <math id="S4.F2.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textit{DDPM}_{past}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">DDPM</mtext><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textit{DDPM}_{past}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.F2.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textit{DDPM}_{fut}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">DDPM</mtext><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textit{DDPM}_{fut}</annotation></semantics></math>. <math id="S4.F2.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textit{DDPM}_{past}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">DDPM</mtext><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textit{DDPM}_{past}</annotation></semantics></math> simultaneously reconstructs the unobserved historical trajectory <math id="S4.F2.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{x}^{ubs}_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>𝐱</mi><mn>0</mn><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x}^{ubs}_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> and estimates its associated uncertainty <math id="S4.F2.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{u}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐮</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{u}</annotation></semantics></math>. Then, <math id="S4.F2.m25" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textit{DDPM}_{fut}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">DDPM</mtext><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textit{DDPM}_{fut}</annotation></semantics></math> predicts the future trajectories <math id="S4.F2.m26" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{y}_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐲</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{y}_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>. Both diffusion models share a common encoder that extracts contextual features <math id="S4.F2.m27" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{h_{1}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐡</mi><mn>𝟏</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{h_{1}}</annotation></semantics></math> from the observed history <math id="S4.F2.m28" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{x}^{obs}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝐱</mi><mrow><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x}^{obs}</annotation></semantics></math>, while <math id="S4.F2.m29" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textit{DDPM}_{fut}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">DDPM</mtext><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textit{DDPM}_{fut}</annotation></semantics></math> employs an additional trajectory encoder to extract features <math id="S4.F2.m30" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{v}_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐯</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{v}_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> from the predicted unobserved historical trajectory <math id="S4.F2.m31" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{x}^{ubs}_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>𝐱</mi><mn>0</mn><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x}^{ubs}_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>. (b) <span id="S4.F2.9" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Dual-head Parameterization Mechanism</span>: The standard noise prediction network is augmented with two output heads, one for predicting noise and the other for predicting variance, which corresponds to aleatoric uncertainty.
(c) <span id="S4.F2.10" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Learnable Temporally Adaptive Noise Scheduling</span>: A gamma module takes <math id="S4.F2.m32" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{u}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐮</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{u}</annotation></semantics></math> and the diffusion step <math id="S4.F2.m33" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math> as inputs and dynamically modulates the noise scale in the forward diffusion process of <math id="S4.F2.m34" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textit{DDPM}_{fut}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">DDPM</mtext><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textit{DDPM}_{fut}</annotation></semantics></math>. Note that the red dashed line indicates the unobserved historical trajectory, with gray shading representing its uncertainty, while the green and blue dashed lines denote the observed history and future trajectories, respectively.
</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S4.SS2.4" class="ltx_text">IV-B</span> </span><span id="S4.SS2.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Dual-head Parameterization</span></h3>

<div id="S4.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this work, to quantify the aleatoric uncertainty of predicted unobservable historical trajectories efficiently, we design a dual-head parameterization mechanism that augment the standard noise prediction network to output two heads:</p>
<table id="S4.E7" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\epsilon_{\theta_{1}}(\mathbf{x}^{ubs}_{m},m,\mathbf{h}_{1}),\bm{\ell}_{\theta_{1}}(\mathbf{x}^{ubs}_{m},m,\mathbf{h}_{1}))," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><msub><mi>θ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>m</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐡</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_bold" mathvariant="bold">ℓ</mi><msub><mi>θ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>m</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐡</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\epsilon_{\theta_{1}}(\mathbf{x}^{ubs}_{m},m,\mathbf{h}_{1}),\bm{\ell}_{\theta_{1}}(\mathbf{x}^{ubs}_{m},m,\mathbf{h}_{1})),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(7)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>θ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the learned parameters of <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textit{DDPM}_{past}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">DDPM</mtext><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textit{DDPM}_{past}</annotation></semantics></math>. The first head, <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon_{\theta_{1}}(\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><msub><mi>θ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon_{\theta_{1}}(\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math>, predicts the scaled Gaussian noise as in standard diffusion models, while the second head, <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\ell}_{\theta_{1}}(\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_bold" mathvariant="bold">ℓ</mi><msub><mi>θ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\ell}_{\theta_{1}}(\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math>, directly predicts the log-variance on per-coordinate basis. For brevity, we abbreviate <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\ell}_{\theta_{1}}(\mathbf{x}^{obs}_{m},m,\mathbf{h}_{1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_bold" mathvariant="bold">ℓ</mi><msub><mi>θ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>m</mi><mrow><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐡</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\ell}_{\theta_{1}}(\mathbf{x}^{obs}_{m},m,\mathbf{h}_{1})</annotation></semantics></math> by <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\ell}_{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_bold" mathvariant="bold">ℓ</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\ell}_{m}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon_{\theta_{1}}(\mathbf{x}^{obs}_{m},m,\mathbf{h}_{1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><msub><mi>θ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>m</mi><mrow><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐡</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon_{\theta_{1}}(\mathbf{x}^{obs}_{m},m,\mathbf{h}_{1})</annotation></semantics></math> by <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\hat{\epsilon}}_{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>m</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\hat{\epsilon}}_{m}</annotation></semantics></math> in what follows. During the inference phase, the dual-head parameterization propagates uncertainty without Monte-Carlo averaging. We update the reverse process as follows:</p>
<table id="S4.EGx3" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S4.E8"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle p_{\theta_{1}}\!\left(x^{\mathrm{ubs}}_{m-1}\mid x^{\mathrm{ubs}}_{m},\,m,\,\mathbf{h}_{1}\right)=\mathcal{N}\!\big(\bm{\mu}_{\theta_{1}},\,\bm{\Sigma}_{\theta_{1}}\big)," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><msub><mi>θ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>ubs</mi></msubsup><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>m</mi><mi>ubs</mi></msubsup><mo rspace="0.337em">,</mo><mi>m</mi><mo rspace="0.337em">,</mo><msub><mi>𝐡</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mpadded style="width:0.798em;" width="0.798em"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi></mpadded><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><msub><mi>𝝁</mi><msub><mi>θ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝚺</mi><msub><mi>θ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msub><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle p_{\theta_{1}}\!\left(x^{\mathrm{ubs}}_{m-1}\mid x^{\mathrm{ubs}}_{m},\,m,\,\mathbf{h}_{1}\right)=\mathcal{N}\!\big(\bm{\mu}_{\theta_{1}},\,\bm{\Sigma}_{\theta_{1}}\big),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(8)</span></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S4.E9"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\bm{\mu}_{\theta_{1}}=\frac{\alpha_{m\mid n}\,\sigma_{n}^{2}}{\sigma_{m}^{2}}\,\mathbf{x}^{\mathrm{ubs}}_{m}+\frac{\sigma_{m\mid n}^{2}\,\alpha_{n}}{\sigma_{m}^{2}}\,\frac{\mathbf{x}^{\mathrm{ubs}}_{m}-\sigma_{m}\,{\hat{\epsilon}}_{m}}{\alpha_{m}}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝝁</mi><msub><mi>θ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo fence="false">|</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mi>m</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>m</mi><mi>ubs</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo fence="false">|</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mi>m</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>m</mi><mi>ubs</mi></msubsup><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mfrac></mstyle></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\bm{\mu}_{\theta_{1}}=\frac{\alpha_{m\mid n}\,\sigma_{n}^{2}}{\sigma_{m}^{2}}\,\mathbf{x}^{\mathrm{ubs}}_{m}+\frac{\sigma_{m\mid n}^{2}\,\alpha_{n}}{\sigma_{m}^{2}}\,\frac{\mathbf{x}^{\mathrm{ubs}}_{m}-\sigma_{m}\,{\hat{\epsilon}}_{m}}{\alpha_{m}},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(9)</span></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S4.E10"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\bm{\Sigma}_{\theta_{1}}=\operatorname{diag}\!\big(\exp(\bm{\ell}_{m})\big)+\frac{\sigma_{n}^{2}\,\sigma_{m\mid n}^{2}}{\sigma_{m}^{2}}\,\bm{I}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝚺</mi><msub><mi>θ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mpadded style="width:1.736em;" width="1.736em"><mi>diag</mi></mpadded><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_bold" mathvariant="bold">ℓ</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo fence="false">|</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mi>m</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝑰</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\bm{\Sigma}_{\theta_{1}}=\operatorname{diag}\!\big(\exp(\bm{\ell}_{m})\big)+\frac{\sigma_{n}^{2}\,\sigma_{m\mid n}^{2}}{\sigma_{m}^{2}}\,\bm{I},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(10)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS2.p1.3" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{diag}(\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>diag</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{diag}(\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the operator that maps a vector to the diagonal matrix formed from its entries, with <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\mu}_{\theta_{1}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝝁</mi><msub><mi>θ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\mu}_{\theta_{1}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\Sigma}_{\theta_{1}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝚺</mi><msub><mi>θ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\Sigma}_{\theta_{1}}</annotation></semantics></math> denoting the predicted mean and covariance of <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textit{DDPM}_{past}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">DDPM</mtext><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textit{DDPM}_{past}</annotation></semantics></math>, respectively. Applying the Tweedie formula <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib53" title="" class="ltx_ref">53</a>]</cite> yields a closed-form variance for the final prediction <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{x}^{ubs}_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>𝐱</mi><mn>0</mn><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x}^{ubs}_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
<table id="S4.EGx4" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S4.E11"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\operatorname{diag}(\mathbf{u})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>diag</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐮</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\operatorname{diag}(\mathbf{u})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E11.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\operatorname{diag}\!\big(\exp(\bm{\ell}_{m})\big)+\frac{\sigma_{n}^{2}\,\sigma_{m\mid n}^{2}}{\sigma_{m}^{2}}\,\bm{I}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mpadded style="width:1.736em;" width="1.736em"><mi>diag</mi></mpadded><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_bold" mathvariant="bold">ℓ</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo fence="false">|</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mi>m</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝑰</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\operatorname{diag}\!\big(\exp(\bm{\ell}_{m})\big)+\frac{\sigma_{n}^{2}\,\sigma_{m\mid n}^{2}}{\sigma_{m}^{2}}\,\bm{I}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(11)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS2.p1.4" class="ltx_p">The first term captures the data-dependent aleatoric uncertainty, whereas the second term accounts for the known noise introduced by the scheduler. To isolate the intrinsic uncertainty, the second term can be subtracted. It is worth noting that the dual-head parameterization mechanism is designed for <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textit{DDPM}_{past}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">DDPM</mtext><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textit{DDPM}_{past}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S4.SS3.4" class="ltx_text">IV-C</span> </span><span id="S4.SS3.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Temporally-Adaptive Learnable Noise Scheduling</span></h3>

<div id="S4.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Conventional diffusion frameworks, with their preset forward noise schedules, often struggle to exploit known data features. To bridge this gap, we design a temporally-adaptive, uncertainty-aware noise schedule inspired by <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib54" title="" class="ltx_ref">54</a>]</cite> that guides the diffusion process to better capture sample-specific structures and improves data distribution modeling. In particular, we leverage the uncertainty of predicted historical trajectories to adapt the noise schedule.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Naturally, the forward process for such noise schedule is defined as:</p>
<table id="S4.EGx5" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S4.E12"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle q(\mathbf{y}_{m}\mid\mathbf{y}_{0},\mathbf{u})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐲</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐲</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐮</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle q(\mathbf{y}_{m}\mid\mathbf{y}_{0},\mathbf{u})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E12.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle:=\mathcal{N}\!\big(\mathbf{y}_{m};\bm{\alpha}_{m}(\mathbf{u})\mathbf{y}_{0},\;\operatorname{diag}(\bm{\sigma}_{m}^{2}(\mathbf{u})\big)." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mpadded style="width:0.798em;" width="0.798em"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi></mpadded><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐲</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>;</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝜶</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐮</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝐲</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><mo rspace="0.447em">,</mo><mrow><mi>diag</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝝈</mi><mi>m</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐮</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">CLOSE</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle:=\mathcal{N}\!\big(\mathbf{y}_{m};\bm{\alpha}_{m}(\mathbf{u})\mathbf{y}_{0},\;\operatorname{diag}(\bm{\sigma}_{m}^{2}(\mathbf{u})\big).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(12)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS3.p2.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{y}_{m},\mathbf{y}_{0}\in\mathbb{R}^{T_{fur}\times 2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐲</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐲</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{y}_{m},\mathbf{y}_{0}\in\mathbb{R}^{T_{fur}\times 2}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\alpha}_{m}(\mathbf{u}),\bm{\sigma}_{m}(\mathbf{u})\in\mathbb{R}^{T_{fut}}_{+}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝜶</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐮</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝝈</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐮</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msubsup><mi>ℝ</mi><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub></msubsup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\alpha}_{m}(\mathbf{u}),\bm{\sigma}_{m}(\mathbf{u})\in\mathbb{R}^{T_{fut}}_{+}</annotation></semantics></math>. Then, the denoising process is given by:</p>
<table id="S4.EGx6" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S4.E13"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle p_{\theta_{2}}(\mathbf{y}_{n}\mid\mathbf{y}_{m},\mathbf{h}_{2},m,\mathbf{u}):=\mathcal{N}\!\big(\bm{\mu}_{\theta_{2}},\,\bm{\Sigma}_{\theta_{2}}\big)," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><msub><mi>θ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐲</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐲</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐡</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐮</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mpadded style="width:0.798em;" width="0.798em"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi></mpadded><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><msub><mi>𝝁</mi><msub><mi>θ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝚺</mi><msub><mi>θ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></msub><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle p_{\theta_{2}}(\mathbf{y}_{n}\mid\mathbf{y}_{m},\mathbf{h}_{2},m,\mathbf{u}):=\mathcal{N}\!\big(\bm{\mu}_{\theta_{2}},\,\bm{\Sigma}_{\theta_{2}}\big),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(13)</span></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S4.E14"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E14.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\bm{\mu}_{\theta_{2}}=\frac{\bm{\alpha}_{m\mid n}(\mathbf{u})\,\bm{\sigma}_{n}^{2}(\mathbf{u})}{\bm{\sigma}_{m}^{2}(\mathbf{u})}\,\mathbf{y}_{m}+\frac{\bm{\sigma}_{m\mid n}^{2}(\mathbf{u})\,\bm{\alpha}_{n}(\mathbf{u})}{\bm{\sigma}_{m}^{2}(\mathbf{u})}\,\mathbf{y}_{\theta}(\mathbf{y}_{m},\mathbf{h}_{2},m,\mathbf{u})," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝝁</mi><msub><mi>θ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>𝜶</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo fence="false">|</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐮</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>𝝈</mi><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐮</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝝈</mi><mi>m</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐮</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝐲</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝝈</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo fence="false">|</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐮</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝜶</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐮</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝝈</mi><mi>m</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐮</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝐲</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐲</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐡</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐮</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\bm{\mu}_{\theta_{2}}=\frac{\bm{\alpha}_{m\mid n}(\mathbf{u})\,\bm{\sigma}_{n}^{2}(\mathbf{u})}{\bm{\sigma}_{m}^{2}(\mathbf{u})}\,\mathbf{y}_{m}+\frac{\bm{\sigma}_{m\mid n}^{2}(\mathbf{u})\,\bm{\alpha}_{n}(\mathbf{u})}{\bm{\sigma}_{m}^{2}(\mathbf{u})}\,\mathbf{y}_{\theta}(\mathbf{y}_{m},\mathbf{h}_{2},m,\mathbf{u}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(14)</span></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S4.E15"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E15.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\bm{\Sigma}_{\theta_{2}}=\frac{\bm{\sigma}_{n}^{2}(\mathbf{u})\,\bm{\sigma}_{m\mid n}^{2}(\mathbf{u})}{\bm{\sigma}_{m}^{2}(\mathbf{u})}\,\bm{I}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝚺</mi><msub><mi>θ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝝈</mi><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐮</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>𝝈</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo fence="false">|</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐮</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝝈</mi><mi>m</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐮</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝑰</mi></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\bm{\Sigma}_{\theta_{2}}=\frac{\bm{\sigma}_{n}^{2}(\mathbf{u})\,\bm{\sigma}_{m\mid n}^{2}(\mathbf{u})}{\bm{\sigma}_{m}^{2}(\mathbf{u})}\,\bm{I}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(15)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS3.p2.3" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>θ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the parameters of <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textit{DDPM}_{fut}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">DDPM</mtext><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textit{DDPM}_{fut}</annotation></semantics></math>. Following <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib54" title="" class="ltx_ref">54</a>]</cite>, we control the the forward dynamic through a bounded log-SNR field <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\gamma}_{\varphi}(\mathbf{u},m)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝜸</mi><mi>φ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐮</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\gamma}_{\varphi}(\mathbf{u},m)</annotation></semantics></math>. Given <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\gamma}_{\varphi}(\mathbf{u},m)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝜸</mi><mi>φ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐮</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\gamma}_{\varphi}(\mathbf{u},m)</annotation></semantics></math>, the diffusion parameters are recovered as <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\alpha}_{m}^{2}(\mathbf{u})=\operatorname{sigmoid}\!\big(-\bm{\gamma}_{\varphi}(\mathbf{u},m)\big)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝜶</mi><mi>m</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐮</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mpadded style="width:3.384em;" width="3.384em"><mi>sigmoid</mi></mpadded><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝜸</mi><mi>φ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐮</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\alpha}_{m}^{2}(\mathbf{u})=\operatorname{sigmoid}\!\big(-\bm{\gamma}_{\varphi}(\mathbf{u},m)\big)</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\sigma}_{m}^{2}(\mathbf{c})=\operatorname{sigmoid}\!\big(\bm{\gamma}_{\varphi}(\mathbf{u},m)\big)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝝈</mi><mi>m</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐜</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mpadded style="width:3.384em;" width="3.384em"><mi>sigmoid</mi></mpadded><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝜸</mi><mi>φ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐮</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\sigma}_{m}^{2}(\mathbf{c})=\operatorname{sigmoid}\!\big(\bm{\gamma}_{\varphi}(\mathbf{u},m)\big)</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\nu}(\mathbf{u},m)=\exp\!\big(-\bm{\gamma}_{\varphi}(\mathbf{u},m)\big)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝝂</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐮</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mpadded style="width:1.370em;" width="1.370em"><mi>exp</mi></mpadded><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝜸</mi><mi>φ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐮</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\nu}(\mathbf{u},m)=\exp\!\big(-\bm{\gamma}_{\varphi}(\mathbf{u},m)\big)</annotation></semantics></math>. Here, <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\gamma}_{\varphi}(\mathbf{u},m)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝜸</mi><mi>φ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐮</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\gamma}_{\varphi}(\mathbf{u},m)</annotation></semantics></math> is defined as:</p>
<table id="S4.E16" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E16.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\gamma}_{\varphi}(\mathbf{u},m)=\gamma_{\text{min}}+(\gamma_{\text{max}}-\gamma_{\text{min}})\frac{f_{\varphi}(\mathbf{u},m)}{f_{\varphi}(\mathbf{u},\,m=M)}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝜸</mi><mi>φ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐮</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>γ</mi><mtext>min</mtext></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>γ</mi><mtext>max</mtext></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>γ</mi><mtext>min</mtext></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>φ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐮</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>φ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐮</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\gamma}_{\varphi}(\mathbf{u},m)=\gamma_{\text{min}}+(\gamma_{\text{max}}-\gamma_{\text{min}})\frac{f_{\varphi}(\mathbf{u},m)}{f_{\varphi}(\mathbf{u},\,m=M)}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(16)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS3.p2.4" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{\varphi}(\mathbf{u},m)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>φ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐮</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{\varphi}(\mathbf{u},m)</annotation></semantics></math> is a monotonic degree 5 polynomial in <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
<table id="S4.EGx7" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S4.Ex1"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle f_{\varphi}(\mathbf{u},m)=\frac{a_{1}^{2}(\mathbf{u})}{5}m^{5}+\frac{a_{1}(\mathbf{u})a_{2}(\mathbf{u})}{2}m^{4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>φ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐮</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><msubsup><mi>a</mi><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐮</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mn>5</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>m</mi><mn>5</mn></msup></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐮</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐮</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>m</mi><mn>4</mn></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle f_{\varphi}(\mathbf{u},m)=\frac{a_{1}^{2}(\mathbf{u})}{5}m^{5}+\frac{a_{1}(\mathbf{u})a_{2}(\mathbf{u})}{2}m^{4}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S4.E17"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E17.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle+\frac{a_{2}^{2}(\mathbf{u})+2a_{1}(\mathbf{u})a_{3}(\mathbf{u})}{3}m^{3}+a_{2}(\mathbf{u})a_{3}(\mathbf{u})m^{2}+a_{3}^{2}(\mathbf{u})m+\mathcal{C}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>a</mi><mn>2</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐮</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐮</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐮</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mn>3</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>m</mi><mn>3</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐮</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐮</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>m</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>a</mi><mn>3</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐮</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle+\frac{a_{2}^{2}(\mathbf{u})+2a_{1}(\mathbf{u})a_{3}(\mathbf{u})}{3}m^{3}+a_{2}(\mathbf{u})a_{3}(\mathbf{u})m^{2}+a_{3}^{2}(\mathbf{u})m+\mathcal{C}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(17)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS3.p2.5" class="ltx_p">This parameterization enforces the boundary conditions
<math id="S4.SS3.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\gamma}_{\varphi}(\mathbf{u},0)=\gamma_{\min}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝜸</mi><mi>φ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐮</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>γ</mi><mi>min</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\gamma}_{\varphi}(\mathbf{u},0)=\gamma_{\min}</annotation></semantics></math> and
<math id="S4.SS3.p2.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\gamma}_{\varphi}(\mathbf{u},M)=\gamma_{\max}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝜸</mi><mi>φ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐮</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>γ</mi><mi>max</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\gamma}_{\varphi}(\mathbf{u},M)=\gamma_{\max}</annotation></semantics></math>.
We implement the coefficient predictor as a neural network comprising two LSTM layers
followed by a two-layer MLP. The network outputs three coefficient vectors
<math id="S4.SS3.p2.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{1},a_{2},a_{3}\in\mathbb{R}^{T_{fut}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{1},a_{2},a_{3}\in\mathbb{R}^{T_{fut}}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Following <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib55" title="" class="ltx_ref">55</a>, <a href="#bib.bib56" title="" class="ltx_ref">56</a>]</cite>, we set <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma_{\min}=-13.30" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>γ</mi><mi>min</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>13.30</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma_{\min}=-13.30</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma_{\max}=5.0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>γ</mi><mi>max</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>5.0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma_{\max}=5.0</annotation></semantics></math>. Here, <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{C}</annotation></semantics></math> denotes a constant.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S4.SS4.4" class="ltx_text">IV-D</span> </span><span id="S4.SS4.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Loss Function</span></h3>

<div id="S4.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The loss function of DDPM is derived from the variational lower bound (VLB) of the negative log-likelihood of the data. <span id="S4.SS4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Diffusion<sup id="S4.SS4.p1.1.1.1" class="ltx_sup">2</sup></span> in this work comprises two sequentially connected diffusion models. Thus, the overall loss function naturally decomposes into two components, each corresponding to one direction of prediction.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS4.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS4.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS4.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Loss for <math id="S4.SS4.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textit{DDPM}_{past}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">DDPM</mtext><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textit{DDPM}_{past}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span> For <span id="S4.SS4.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">backward prediction</span>, we design a dual-head parameterization mechanism that enables the diffusion model to estimate both the mean and aleatoric uncertainty. Concretely, the one-step reverse transition in the past branch is specified as Eq. <a href="#S4.E8" title="In IV-B Dual-head Parameterization ‣ IV Methods ‣ Diffusion2: Dual Diffusion Model with Uncertainty-Aware Adaptive Noise for Momentary Trajectory Prediction" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a>, with the <math id="S4.SS4.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϵ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon</annotation></semantics></math>-parameterized mean and diagonal covariance given by Eqs. <a href="#S4.E9" title="In IV-B Dual-head Parameterization ‣ IV Methods ‣ Diffusion2: Dual Diffusion Model with Uncertainty-Aware Adaptive Noise for Momentary Trajectory Prediction" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9</span></a> and <a href="#S4.E10" title="In IV-B Dual-head Parameterization ‣ IV Methods ‣ Diffusion2: Dual Diffusion Model with Uncertainty-Aware Adaptive Noise for Momentary Trajectory Prediction" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">10</span></a>. Naturally, the evidence lower bound (ELBO) contribution at step <math id="S4.SS4.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math> is the Gaussian negative log-likelihood:</p>
<table id="S4.E18" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E18.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}^{\text{NLL}}_{m}=\tfrac{1}{2}\!\left(\mathbf{x}^{\mathrm{ubs}}_{m-1}-\bm{\mu}_{\theta_{1}}\right)^{\!\top}\bm{\Sigma}_{\theta_{1}}^{-1}\left(\mathbf{x}^{\mathrm{ubs}}_{m-1}-\bm{\mu}_{\theta_{1}}\right)+\tfrac{1}{2}\log\!\left|\bm{\Sigma}_{\theta_{1}}\right|+\mathcal{C}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>m</mi><mtext>NLL</mtext></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="false"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝐱</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>ubs</mi></msubsup><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>𝝁</mi><msub><mi>θ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msub></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>𝚺</mi><msub><mi>θ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝐱</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>ubs</mi></msubsup><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>𝝁</mi><msub><mi>θ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msub></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="false"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mpadded style="width:1.146em;" width="1.146em"><mi>log</mi></mpadded><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>|</mo><msub><mi>𝚺</mi><msub><mi>θ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msub><mo>|</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}^{\text{NLL}}_{m}=\tfrac{1}{2}\!\left(\mathbf{x}^{\mathrm{ubs}}_{m-1}-\bm{\mu}_{\theta_{1}}\right)^{\!\top}\bm{\Sigma}_{\theta_{1}}^{-1}\left(\mathbf{x}^{\mathrm{ubs}}_{m-1}-\bm{\mu}_{\theta_{1}}\right)+\tfrac{1}{2}\log\!\left|\bm{\Sigma}_{\theta_{1}}\right|+\mathcal{C},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(18)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS4.p2.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S4.SS4.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{C}</annotation></semantics></math> also collects terms independent of the learnable parameters. Replacing <math id="S4.SS4.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\hat{\epsilon}}_{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>m</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\hat{\epsilon}}_{m}</annotation></semantics></math> in Eq. <a href="#S4.E9" title="In IV-B Dual-head Parameterization ‣ IV Methods ‣ Diffusion2: Dual Diffusion Model with Uncertainty-Aware Adaptive Noise for Momentary Trajectory Prediction" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9</span></a> with the true noise <math id="S4.SS4.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϵ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon</annotation></semantics></math> defines the posterior mean <math id="S4.SS4.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\mu}^{\star}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝝁</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\mu}^{\star}</annotation></semantics></math>, and their difference is:</p>
<table id="S4.E19" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E19.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\mu}^{\star}-\bm{\mu}_{\theta_{1}}=-\,\frac{\sigma_{m\mid n}^{2}\sigma_{n}\,}{\alpha_{m}\,\sigma_{m}}\,\big(\epsilon-{\hat{\epsilon}}_{m}\big)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>𝝁</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>𝝁</mi><msub><mi>θ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mfrac><mrow><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo fence="false">|</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>−</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\mu}^{\star}-\bm{\mu}_{\theta_{1}}=-\,\frac{\sigma_{m\mid n}^{2}\sigma_{n}\,}{\alpha_{m}\,\sigma_{m}}\,\big(\epsilon-{\hat{\epsilon}}_{m}\big).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(19)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS4.p2.3" class="ltx_p">Using (<a href="#S4.E19" title="In IV-D Loss Function ‣ IV Methods ‣ Diffusion2: Dual Diffusion Model with Uncertainty-Aware Adaptive Noise for Momentary Trajectory Prediction" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">19</span></a>) in the quadratic form of (<a href="#S4.E18" title="In IV-D Loss Function ‣ IV Methods ‣ Diffusion2: Dual Diffusion Model with Uncertainty-Aware Adaptive Noise for Momentary Trajectory Prediction" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">18</span></a>) and the diagonal structure of
<math id="S4.SS4.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\Sigma}_{\theta_{1}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝚺</mi><msub><mi>θ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\Sigma}_{\theta_{1}}</annotation></semantics></math>, all schedule-only factors collapse into constants, leaving the
parameter-dependent precision weighting <math id="S4.SS4.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\exp(-\bm{\ell}_{m})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_bold" mathvariant="bold">ℓ</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\exp(-\bm{\ell}_{m})</annotation></semantics></math> on the <math id="S4.SS4.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϵ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon</annotation></semantics></math>-residual. The
log-determinant simplifies analogously since <math id="S4.SS4.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\Sigma}_{\theta_{1}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝚺</mi><msub><mi>θ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\Sigma}_{\theta_{1}}</annotation></semantics></math> is diagonal. Discarding
terms independent of <math id="S4.SS4.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon_{\theta_{1}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><msub><mi>θ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon_{\theta_{1}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS4.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\ell}_{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_bold" mathvariant="bold">ℓ</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\ell}_{m}</annotation></semantics></math> yields the heteroscedastic Gaussian negative log-likelihood we optimize:</p>
<table id="S4.E20" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E20.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{1}=\frac{1}{2}\,\exp(-\bm{\ell}_{m})\,\big\|{\epsilon}-\epsilon_{\theta_{1}}(\mathbf{x}^{\mathrm{ubs}}_{m},\,m,\,\mathbf{h}_{1})\big\|_{2}^{2}\;+\;\frac{1}{2}\,\bm{\ell}_{m}\,." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0.337em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_bold" mathvariant="bold">ℓ</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em" stretchy="true">‖</mo><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><msub><mi>θ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>m</mi><mi>ubs</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐡</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em" stretchy="true">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow><mo rspace="0.502em">+</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_bold" mathvariant="bold">ℓ</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{1}=\frac{1}{2}\,\exp(-\bm{\ell}_{m})\,\big\|{\epsilon}-\epsilon_{\theta_{1}}(\mathbf{x}^{\mathrm{ubs}}_{m},\,m,\,\mathbf{h}_{1})\big\|_{2}^{2}\;+\;\frac{1}{2}\,\bm{\ell}_{m}\,.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(20)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS4.p2.4" class="ltx_p">The first term performs precision-weighted regression in <math id="S4.SS4.p2.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϵ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon</annotation></semantics></math>-space, while the second is the
<math id="S4.SS4.p2.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\log|\bm{\Sigma}_{\theta_{1}}|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>𝚺</mi><msub><mi>θ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\log|\bm{\Sigma}_{\theta_{1}}|</annotation></semantics></math> regularizer that prevents vanishing-variance (overconfident) solutions.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS4.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS4.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS4.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Loss for <math id="S4.SS4.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textit{DDPM}_{fut}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">DDPM</mtext><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textit{DDPM}_{fut}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span> For forward prediction, we parameterize the one-step conditional as in Eq. <a href="#S4.E13" title="In IV-C Temporally-Adaptive Learnable Noise Scheduling ‣ IV Methods ‣ Diffusion2: Dual Diffusion Model with Uncertainty-Aware Adaptive Noise for Momentary Trajectory Prediction" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">13</span></a>, with
<math id="S4.SS4.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\mu}_{\theta_{2}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝝁</mi><msub><mi>θ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\mu}_{\theta_{2}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS4.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\Sigma}_{\theta_{2}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝚺</mi><msub><mi>θ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\Sigma}_{\theta_{2}}</annotation></semantics></math> given by
Eqs. <a href="#S4.E14" title="In IV-C Temporally-Adaptive Learnable Noise Scheduling ‣ IV Methods ‣ Diffusion2: Dual Diffusion Model with Uncertainty-Aware Adaptive Noise for Momentary Trajectory Prediction" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">14</span></a> and <a href="#S4.E15" title="In IV-C Temporally-Adaptive Learnable Noise Scheduling ‣ IV Methods ‣ Diffusion2: Dual Diffusion Model with Uncertainty-Aware Adaptive Noise for Momentary Trajectory Prediction" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">15</span></a>. Under our context-conditioned Gaussian
transition definition, the forward process is</p>
<table id="S4.E21" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E21.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q(\mathbf{y}_{m}\mid\mathbf{y}_{n},\mathbf{u}):=\mathcal{N}\!\big(\bm{\alpha}_{m\mid n}(\mathbf{u})\,\mathbf{y}_{n},\;\bm{\sigma}^{2}_{m\mid n}(\mathbf{u})\,\bm{I}\big)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐲</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐲</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐮</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mpadded style="width:0.798em;" width="0.798em"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi></mpadded><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝜶</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo fence="false">|</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐮</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝐲</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝝈</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo fence="false">|</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐮</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝑰</mi></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q(\mathbf{y}_{m}\mid\mathbf{y}_{n},\mathbf{u}):=\mathcal{N}\!\big(\bm{\alpha}_{m\mid n}(\mathbf{u})\,\mathbf{y}_{n},\;\bm{\sigma}^{2}_{m\mid n}(\mathbf{u})\,\bm{I}\big).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(21)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS4.p3.2" class="ltx_p">The corresponding conditional distribution of <math id="S4.SS4.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{y}_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐲</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{y}_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> given <math id="S4.SS4.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{y}_{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐲</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{y}_{m}</annotation></semantics></math> and the clean target
<math id="S4.SS4.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{y}_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐲</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{y}_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> is</p>
<table id="S4.EGx8" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S4.E22"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E22.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle q(\mathbf{y}_{n}\mid\mathbf{y}_{m},\mathbf{y}_{0},\mathbf{u}):=\mathcal{N}\!\big(\bm{\mu}_{q},\;\bm{\Sigma}_{q}\big)," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐲</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐲</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐲</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐮</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mpadded style="width:0.798em;" width="0.798em"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi></mpadded><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><msub><mi>𝝁</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝚺</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle q(\mathbf{y}_{n}\mid\mathbf{y}_{m},\mathbf{y}_{0},\mathbf{u}):=\mathcal{N}\!\big(\bm{\mu}_{q},\;\bm{\Sigma}_{q}\big),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(22)</span></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S4.E23"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E23.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\bm{\mu}_{q}=\frac{\bm{\alpha}_{m\mid n}(\mathbf{u})\,\bm{\sigma}_{n}^{2}(\mathbf{u})}{\bm{\sigma}_{m}^{2}(\mathbf{u})}\,\mathbf{y}_{m}+\frac{\bm{\sigma}_{m\mid n}^{2}(\mathbf{u})\,\bm{\alpha}_{n}(\mathbf{u})}{\bm{\sigma}_{m}^{2}(\mathbf{u})}\,\mathbf{y}_{0}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝝁</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>𝜶</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo fence="false">|</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐮</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>𝝈</mi><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐮</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝝈</mi><mi>m</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐮</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝐲</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝝈</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo fence="false">|</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐮</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝜶</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐮</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝝈</mi><mi>m</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐮</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝐲</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\bm{\mu}_{q}=\frac{\bm{\alpha}_{m\mid n}(\mathbf{u})\,\bm{\sigma}_{n}^{2}(\mathbf{u})}{\bm{\sigma}_{m}^{2}(\mathbf{u})}\,\mathbf{y}_{m}+\frac{\bm{\sigma}_{m\mid n}^{2}(\mathbf{u})\,\bm{\alpha}_{n}(\mathbf{u})}{\bm{\sigma}_{m}^{2}(\mathbf{u})}\,\mathbf{y}_{0},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(23)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS4.p3.3" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S4.SS4.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\Sigma}_{q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝚺</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\Sigma}_{q}</annotation></semantics></math> equals Eq. <a href="#S4.E15" title="In IV-C Temporally-Adaptive Learnable Noise Scheduling ‣ IV Methods ‣ Diffusion2: Dual Diffusion Model with Uncertainty-Aware Adaptive Noise for Momentary Trajectory Prediction" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">15</span></a>, i.e.,
<math id="S4.SS4.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\Sigma}_{q}=\bm{\Sigma}_{\theta_{2}}=\frac{\bm{\sigma}_{n}^{2}(\mathbf{u})\,\bm{\sigma}_{m\mid n}^{2}(\mathbf{u})}{\bm{\sigma}_{m}^{2}(\mathbf{u})}\,\bm{I}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝚺</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>𝚺</mi><msub><mi>θ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝝈</mi><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐮</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>𝝈</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo fence="false">|</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐮</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝝈</mi><mi>m</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐮</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝑰</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\Sigma}_{q}=\bm{\Sigma}_{\theta_{2}}=\frac{\bm{\sigma}_{n}^{2}(\mathbf{u})\,\bm{\sigma}_{m\mid n}^{2}(\mathbf{u})}{\bm{\sigma}_{m}^{2}(\mathbf{u})}\,\bm{I}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS4.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS4.p4.1" class="ltx_p">With matching covariances, the ELBO term reduces to the KL between two Gaussians that differ only in
their means:</p>
<table id="S4.EGx9" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S4.E24"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E24.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle D_{\mathrm{KL}}\!\big(q(\mathbf{y}_{n}\!\mid\!\mathbf{y}_{m},\mathbf{y}_{0},\mathbf{u})\,\big\|\,p_{\theta_{2}}(\mathbf{y}_{n}\!\mid\!\mathbf{y}_{m},\mathbf{h}_{2},m,\mathbf{u})\big)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>KL</mi></msub><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mi>q</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐲</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mpadded style="width:0.219em;" width="0.219em"><mo lspace="0em">∣</mo></mpadded><msub><mi>𝐲</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐲</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐮</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" mathsize="1.200em" rspace="0.337em">∥</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><msub><mi>θ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐲</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mpadded style="width:0.219em;" width="0.219em"><mo lspace="0em">∣</mo></mpadded><msub><mi>𝐲</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐡</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐮</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle D_{\mathrm{KL}}\!\big(q(\mathbf{y}_{n}\!\mid\!\mathbf{y}_{m},\mathbf{y}_{0},\mathbf{u})\,\big\|\,p_{\theta_{2}}(\mathbf{y}_{n}\!\mid\!\mathbf{y}_{m},\mathbf{h}_{2},m,\mathbf{u})\big)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(24)</span></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S4.Ex2"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\tfrac{1}{2}\!\left(\bm{\mu}_{\theta_{2}}-\bm{\mu}_{q}\right)^{\!\top}\bm{\Sigma}_{\theta_{2}}^{-1}\left(\bm{\mu}_{\theta_{2}}-\bm{\mu}_{q}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝝁</mi><msub><mi>θ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>𝝁</mi><mi>q</mi></msub></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>𝚺</mi><msub><mi>θ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝝁</mi><msub><mi>θ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>𝝁</mi><mi>q</mi></msub></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\tfrac{1}{2}\!\left(\bm{\mu}_{\theta_{2}}-\bm{\mu}_{q}\right)^{\!\top}\bm{\Sigma}_{\theta_{2}}^{-1}\left(\bm{\mu}_{\theta_{2}}-\bm{\mu}_{q}\right)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S4.Ex3"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\tfrac{1}{2}\!\left(\mathbf{y}_{0}-\mathbf{y}_{\theta_{2}}(\mathbf{y}_{m},\mathbf{h}_{2},m,\mathbf{u})\right)^{\!\top}\mathbf{J}_{0\mid m}(\mathbf{u},m)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐲</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐲</mi><msub><mi>θ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐲</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐡</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐮</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝐉</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo fence="false">|</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐮</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\tfrac{1}{2}\!\left(\mathbf{y}_{0}-\mathbf{y}_{\theta_{2}}(\mathbf{y}_{m},\mathbf{h}_{2},m,\mathbf{u})\right)^{\!\top}\mathbf{J}_{0\mid m}(\mathbf{u},m)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S4.E25"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E25.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\quad\times\left(\mathbf{y}_{0}-\mathbf{y}_{\theta_{2}}(\mathbf{y}_{m},\mathbf{h}_{2},m,\mathbf{u})\right)," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="1.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐲</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐲</mi><msub><mi>θ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐲</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐡</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐮</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\quad\times\left(\mathbf{y}_{0}-\mathbf{y}_{\theta_{2}}(\mathbf{y}_{m},\mathbf{h}_{2},m,\mathbf{u})\right),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(25)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS4.p4.2" class="ltx_p">because</p>
<table id="S4.EGx10" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S4.E26"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E26.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\bm{\mu}_{\theta_{2}}-\bm{\mu}_{q}=\frac{\bm{\sigma}_{m\mid n}^{2}(\mathbf{u})\,\bm{\alpha}_{n}(\mathbf{u})}{\bm{\sigma}_{m}^{2}(\mathbf{u})}\,\big(\mathbf{y}_{\theta_{2}}(\mathbf{y}_{m},\mathbf{h}_{2},m,\mathbf{u})-\mathbf{y}_{0}\big)," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝝁</mi><msub><mi>θ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>𝝁</mi><mi>q</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝝈</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo fence="false">|</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐮</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝜶</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐮</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝝈</mi><mi>m</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐮</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝐲</mi><msub><mi>θ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐲</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐡</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐮</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>𝐲</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\bm{\mu}_{\theta_{2}}-\bm{\mu}_{q}=\frac{\bm{\sigma}_{m\mid n}^{2}(\mathbf{u})\,\bm{\alpha}_{n}(\mathbf{u})}{\bm{\sigma}_{m}^{2}(\mathbf{u})}\,\big(\mathbf{y}_{\theta_{2}}(\mathbf{y}_{m},\mathbf{h}_{2},m,\mathbf{u})-\mathbf{y}_{0}\big),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(26)</span></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S4.E27"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E27.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\qquad\bm{\Sigma}_{\theta_{2}}^{-1}=\frac{\bm{\sigma}_{m}^{2}(\mathbf{u})}{\bm{\sigma}_{n}^{2}(\mathbf{u})\,\bm{\sigma}_{m\mid n}^{2}(\mathbf{u})}\,\mathbf{I}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝚺</mi><msub><mi>θ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝝈</mi><mi>m</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐮</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝝈</mi><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐮</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>𝝈</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo fence="false">|</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐮</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐈</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\qquad\bm{\Sigma}_{\theta_{2}}^{-1}=\frac{\bm{\sigma}_{m}^{2}(\mathbf{u})}{\bm{\sigma}_{n}^{2}(\mathbf{u})\,\bm{\sigma}_{m\mid n}^{2}(\mathbf{u})}\,\mathbf{I},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(27)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS4.p4.3" class="ltx_p">and we define the (context-conditioned) posterior precision as follows:</p>
<table id="S4.EGx11" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S4.Ex4"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathbf{J}_{0\mid m}(\mathbf{u},m)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐉</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo fence="false">|</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐮</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathbf{J}_{0\mid m}(\mathbf{u},m)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle:=\frac{\bm{\alpha}_{n}(\mathbf{u})^{2}\,\bm{\sigma}_{m\mid n}(\mathbf{u})^{2}}{\bm{\sigma}_{n}(\mathbf{u})^{2}\,\bm{\sigma}_{m}(\mathbf{u})^{2}}\;\bm{I}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>𝜶</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐮</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝝈</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo fence="false">|</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐮</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝝈</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐮</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝝈</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐮</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0.280em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝑰</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle:=\frac{\bm{\alpha}_{n}(\mathbf{u})^{2}\,\bm{\sigma}_{m\mid n}(\mathbf{u})^{2}}{\bm{\sigma}_{n}(\mathbf{u})^{2}\,\bm{\sigma}_{m}(\mathbf{u})^{2}}\;\bm{I}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S4.E28"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E28.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\;\equiv\;\operatorname{diag}\!\big(\bm{\nu}(\mathbf{u},n)-\bm{\nu}(\mathbf{u},m)\big)." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.558em" rspace="0.558em">≡</mo><mrow><mpadded style="width:1.736em;" width="1.736em"><mi>diag</mi></mpadded><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>𝝂</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐮</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>𝝂</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐮</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\;\equiv\;\operatorname{diag}\!\big(\bm{\nu}(\mathbf{u},n)-\bm{\nu}(\mathbf{u},m)\big).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(28)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS4.p4.4" class="ltx_p">Dropping constants that are independent of <math id="S4.SS4.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>θ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> and taking the expectation over timesteps
(e.g., by uniformly sampling <math id="S4.SS4.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math>) yields the training loss used for the forward branch:</p>
<table id="S4.EGx12" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S4.E29"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E29.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathcal{L}_{2}=" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mphantom></mphantom></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathcal{L}_{2}=</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E29.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle\frac{1}{2}\,\mathbb{E}\Bigg[\sum_{m=1}^{M}\big(\mathbf{y}_{0}-\mathbf{y}_{\theta_{2}}(\mathbf{y}_{m},\mathbf{h}_{2},m,\mathbf{u})\big)^{\!\top}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mo maxsize="2.600em" minsize="2.600em">[</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>M</mi></munderover></mstyle><msup><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐲</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>𝐲</mi><msub><mi>θ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐲</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐡</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐮</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\frac{1}{2}\,\mathbb{E}\Bigg[\sum_{m=1}^{M}\big(\mathbf{y}_{0}-\mathbf{y}_{\theta_{2}}(\mathbf{y}_{m},\mathbf{h}_{2},m,\mathbf{u})\big)^{\!\top}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(29)</span></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S4.Ex5"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex5.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle\times\operatorname{diag}(\bm{\nu}(\mathbf{u},n)-\bm{\nu}(\mathbf{u},m))\big(\mathbf{y}_{0}-\mathbf{y}_{\theta_{2}}(\mathbf{y}_{m},\mathbf{h}_{2},m,\mathbf{u})\big)\Bigg]." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>diag</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝝂</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐮</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>𝝂</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐮</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐲</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>𝐲</mi><msub><mi>θ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐲</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐡</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐮</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow><mo maxsize="2.600em" minsize="2.600em">]</mo><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\times\operatorname{diag}(\bm{\nu}(\mathbf{u},n)-\bm{\nu}(\mathbf{u},m))\big(\mathbf{y}_{0}-\mathbf{y}_{\theta_{2}}(\mathbf{y}_{m},\mathbf{h}_{2},m,\mathbf{u})\big)\Bigg].</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS4.p4.5" class="ltx_p">Thus, the total loss function <math id="S4.SS4.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}=\mathcal{L}_{1}+\mathcal{L}_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}=\mathcal{L}_{1}+\mathcal{L}_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>, and the training phase is illustrated in Algorithm <a href="#alg1" title="Algorithm 1 ‣ IV-D Loss Function ‣ IV Methods ‣ Diffusion2: Dual Diffusion Model with Uncertainty-Aware Adaptive Noise for Momentary Trajectory Prediction" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="alg1" class="ltx_float ltx_float_algorithm ltx_framed ltx_framed_top">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_float"><span id="alg1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Algorithm 1</span> </span> Pseudocode for <span id="alg1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Diffusion<sup id="alg1.4.1" class="ltx_sup">2</sup></span> Training Phase</figcaption>
<div id="alg1.5" class="ltx_listing ltx_framed ltx_framed_topbottom ltx_listing">
<div id="alg1.l1" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">1:</span></span>
              
            <span id="alg1.l1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">while</span> Model not converged <span id="alg1.l1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">do</span>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l2" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l2.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">2:</span></span>
              
              Sample frames <math id="alg1.l2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\big(\mathbf{x}^{obs},\,\mathbf{x}^{ubs},\,\mathbf{y})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><msup><mi>𝐱</mi><mrow><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>𝐱</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐲</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\big(\mathbf{x}^{obs},\,\mathbf{x}^{ubs},\,\mathbf{y})</annotation></semantics></math> from dataset

</div>
<div id="alg1.l3" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l3.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">3:</span></span>
              
              Sample <math id="alg1.l3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m\sim\mathrm{Uniform}(1,M)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi>Uniform</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m\sim\mathrm{Uniform}(1,M)</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l4" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l4.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">4:</span></span>
              
              Encode: <math id="alg1.l4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{h}_{1}\leftarrow\mathbf{x}^{obs}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐡</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><msup><mi>𝐱</mi><mrow><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{h}_{1}\leftarrow\mathbf{x}^{obs}</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l5" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l5.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">5:</span></span>
              
              Calculate the loss <math id="alg1.l5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> using Eq. <a href="#S4.E20" title="In IV-D Loss Function ‣ IV Methods ‣ Diffusion2: Dual Diffusion Model with Uncertainty-Aware Adaptive Noise for Momentary Trajectory Prediction" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">20</span></a>.

</div>
<div id="alg1.l6" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l6.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">6:</span></span>
              
              Sample <math id="alg1.l6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{x}^{ubs}_{M}\sim\mathcal{N}(\mathbf{0},\bm{I})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>M</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>𝟎</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>𝑰</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x}^{ubs}_{M}\sim\mathcal{N}(\mathbf{0},\bm{I})</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l7" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l7.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">7:</span></span>
              
              <span id="alg1.l7.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">for</span> <math id="alg1.l7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m=\{M,\ldots,1\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m=\{M,\ldots,1\}</annotation></semantics></math> <span id="alg1.l7.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">do</span>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l8" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l8.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">8:</span></span>
              
               Sample <math id="alg1.l8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\epsilon}\sim\mathcal{N}(\mathbf{0},\bm{I})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>𝟎</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>𝑰</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\epsilon}\sim\mathcal{N}(\mathbf{0},\bm{I})</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l9" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l9.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">9:</span></span>
              
               Sample <math id="alg1.l9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\epsilon^{\prime}}\sim\mathcal{N}(\mathbf{0},\bm{I})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>𝟎</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>𝑰</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\epsilon^{\prime}}\sim\mathcal{N}(\mathbf{0},\bm{I})</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l10" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l10.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">10:</span></span>
              
               <math id="alg1.l10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{x}_{m-1}^{ubs}=\bm{\mu}_{\theta_{1}}+\exp(\frac{1}{2}\bm{\ell}_{m})\odot\epsilon+\frac{\sigma_{n}\sigma_{m\mid n}}{\sigma_{m}}\epsilon^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝐱</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝝁</mi><msub><mi>θ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_bold" mathvariant="bold">ℓ</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⊙</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo fence="false">|</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x}_{m-1}^{ubs}=\bm{\mu}_{\theta_{1}}+\exp(\frac{1}{2}\bm{\ell}_{m})\odot\epsilon+\frac{\sigma_{n}\sigma_{m\mid n}}{\sigma_{m}}\epsilon^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l11" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l11.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">11:</span></span>
              
              <span id="alg1.l11.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">end</span> <span id="alg1.l11.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">for</span>
</div>
<div id="alg1.l12" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l12.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">12:</span></span>
              
              Calculate <math id="alg1.l12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{u}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐮</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{u}</annotation></semantics></math> using Eq. <a href="#S4.E11" title="In IV-B Dual-head Parameterization ‣ IV Methods ‣ Diffusion2: Dual Diffusion Model with Uncertainty-Aware Adaptive Noise for Momentary Trajectory Prediction" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">11</span></a>.

</div>
<div id="alg1.l13" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l13.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">13:</span></span>
              
              Encode: <math id="alg1.l13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{v}_{1}\leftarrow\mathbf{x}^{\text{ubs}}_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐯</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐱</mi><mn>0</mn><mtext>ubs</mtext></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{v}_{1}\leftarrow\mathbf{x}^{\text{ubs}}_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>,

</div>
<div id="alg1.l14" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l14.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">14:</span></span>
              
              Concatenate: <math id="alg1.l14.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{h}_{2}\leftarrow\mathbf{v}_{1}\oplus\mathbf{h}_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐡</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐯</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>⊕</mo><msub><mi>𝐡</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{h}_{2}\leftarrow\mathbf{v}_{1}\oplus\mathbf{h}_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l15" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l15.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">15:</span></span>
              
              Sample <math id="alg1.l15.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m\sim\mathrm{Uniform}(1,M)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi>Uniform</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m\sim\mathrm{Uniform}(1,M)</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l16" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l16.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">16:</span></span>
              
              Calculate the loss <math id="alg1.l16.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> using Eq. <a href="#S4.E29" title="In IV-D Loss Function ‣ IV Methods ‣ Diffusion2: Dual Diffusion Model with Uncertainty-Aware Adaptive Noise for Momentary Trajectory Prediction" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">29</span></a>.

</div>
<div id="alg1.l17" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l17.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">17:</span></span>
              
              Take gradient descent step on <math id="alg1.l17.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nabla(\mathcal{L}=\mathcal{L}_{1}+\mathcal{L}_{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>∇</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nabla(\mathcal{L}=\mathcal{L}_{1}+\mathcal{L}_{2})</annotation></semantics></math>, and 
</div>
<div id="alg1.l18" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l18.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">18:</span></span>
              
               then update the model’s parameters.

</div>
<div id="alg1.l19" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l19.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">19:</span></span>
              
            <span id="alg1.l19.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">end</span> <span id="alg1.l19.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">while</span>
</div>
</div>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS5" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S4.SS5.4" class="ltx_text">IV-E</span> </span><span id="S4.SS5.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Model inference</span></h3>

<div id="S4.SS5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The preceding sections detailed the model architecture and training procedure. Once training is complete, we can use <span id="S4.SS5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Diffusion<sup id="S4.SS5.p1.1.1.1" class="ltx_sup"><span id="S4.SS5.p1.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">2</span></sup></span> to generate future pedestrian trajectories. In the first stage, <math id="S4.SS5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textit{DDPM}_{\text{past}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">DDPM</mtext><mtext>past</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textit{DDPM}_{\text{past}}</annotation></semantics></math> performs denoising to obtain plausible unobserved trajectories from pure Gaussian noise <math id="S4.SS5.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{x}_{M}^{ubs}\sim\mathcal{N}(\mathbf{0},\bm{I})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>M</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>𝟎</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>𝑰</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x}_{M}^{ubs}\sim\mathcal{N}(\mathbf{0},\bm{I})</annotation></semantics></math>. The denoising process proceeds as follows:</p>
<table id="S4.EGx13" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S4.E30"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E30.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathbf{x}_{m-1}^{ubs}=\bm{\mu}_{\theta_{1}}+\exp(\frac{1}{2}\bm{\ell}_{m})\odot\epsilon+\frac{\sigma_{n}\sigma_{m\mid n}}{\sigma_{m}}\epsilon^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝐱</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝝁</mi><msub><mi>θ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_bold" mathvariant="bold">ℓ</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⊙</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo fence="false">|</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathbf{x}_{m-1}^{ubs}=\bm{\mu}_{\theta_{1}}+\exp(\frac{1}{2}\bm{\ell}_{m})\odot\epsilon+\frac{\sigma_{n}\sigma_{m\mid n}}{\sigma_{m}}\epsilon^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(30)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS5.p1.2" class="ltx_p">Where <math id="S4.SS5.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϵ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS5.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon^{\prime}\sim\mathcal{N}(\mathbf{0},\bm{I})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>𝟎</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>𝑰</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon^{\prime}\sim\mathcal{N}(\mathbf{0},\bm{I})</annotation></semantics></math>. Meanwhile, leveraging the Tweedie formula <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib53" title="" class="ltx_ref">53</a>]</cite>, we can obtain a closed-form variance for the final prediction <math id="S4.SS5.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{x}_{0}^{ubs}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>𝐱</mi><mn>0</mn><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x}_{0}^{ubs}</annotation></semantics></math>, as shown in Eq. <a href="#S4.E11" title="In IV-B Dual-head Parameterization ‣ IV Methods ‣ Diffusion2: Dual Diffusion Model with Uncertainty-Aware Adaptive Noise for Momentary Trajectory Prediction" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">11</span></a>. In the second stage, we employ the <math id="S4.SS5.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textit{DDPM}_{\text{future}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">DDPM</mtext><mtext>future</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textit{DDPM}_{\text{future}}</annotation></semantics></math> denoising procedure to forecast plausible future trajectories from a pure Gaussian noise <math id="S4.SS5.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{y}_{M}\sim\mathcal{N}(\mathbf{0},\bm{I})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐲</mi><mi>M</mi></msub><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>𝟎</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>𝑰</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{y}_{M}\sim\mathcal{N}(\mathbf{0},\bm{I})</annotation></semantics></math>. Concretely, starting from <math id="S4.SS5.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{y}_{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐲</mi><mi>M</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{y}_{M}</annotation></semantics></math>, we iterate as follows:</p>
<table id="S4.EGx14" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S4.E31"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E31.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathbf{y}_{m-1}=\bm{\mu}_{\theta_{2}}+\frac{\bm{\sigma}_{n}(\mathbf{u})\bm{\sigma}_{m\mid n}(\mathbf{u})}{\bm{\sigma}_{m}(\mathbf{u})}\epsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐲</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝝁</mi><msub><mi>θ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>𝝈</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐮</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝝈</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo fence="false">|</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐮</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝝈</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐮</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathbf{y}_{m-1}=\bm{\mu}_{\theta_{2}}+\frac{\bm{\sigma}_{n}(\mathbf{u})\bm{\sigma}_{m\mid n}(\mathbf{u})}{\bm{\sigma}_{m}(\mathbf{u})}\epsilon</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(31)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS5.p1.3" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S4.SS5.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon\sim\mathcal{N}(\mathbf{0},\bm{I})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>𝟎</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>𝑰</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon\sim\mathcal{N}(\mathbf{0},\bm{I})</annotation></semantics></math>. To summarize, the inference procedure is presented in Algorithm <a href="#alg2" title="Algorithm 2 ‣ IV-E Model inference ‣ IV Methods ‣ Diffusion2: Dual Diffusion Model with Uncertainty-Aware Adaptive Noise for Momentary Trajectory Prediction" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="alg2" class="ltx_float ltx_float_algorithm ltx_framed ltx_framed_top">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_float"><span id="alg2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Algorithm 2</span> </span> Pseudocode for <span id="alg2.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Diffusion<sup id="alg2.4.1" class="ltx_sup">2</sup></span> Sampling Phase</figcaption>
<div id="alg2.5" class="ltx_listing ltx_framed ltx_framed_topbottom ltx_listing">
<div id="alg2.l1" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg2.l1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">1:</span></span>
              
            <span id="alg2.l1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Input:</span> observed frames <math id="alg2.l1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{x}^{obs}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝐱</mi><mrow><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x}^{obs}</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg2.l2" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg2.l2.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">2:</span></span>
              
            <span id="alg2.l2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Output:</span> Future trajectories <math id="alg2.l2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐲</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{y}</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg2.l3" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg2.l3.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">3:</span></span>
              
            Sample <math id="alg2.l3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{x}^{ubs}_{M}\sim\mathcal{N}(\mathbf{0},\bm{I})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>M</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>𝟎</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>𝑰</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x}^{ubs}_{M}\sim\mathcal{N}(\mathbf{0},\bm{I})</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg2.l4" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg2.l4.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">4:</span></span>
              
            Encode: <math id="alg2.l4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{h}_{1}\leftarrow\mathbf{x}^{obs}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐡</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><msup><mi>𝐱</mi><mrow><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{h}_{1}\leftarrow\mathbf{x}^{obs}</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg2.l5" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg2.l5.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">5:</span></span>
              
            Same as Alg. <a href="#alg1" title="Algorithm 1 ‣ IV-D Loss Function ‣ IV Methods ‣ Diffusion2: Dual Diffusion Model with Uncertainty-Aware Adaptive Noise for Momentary Trajectory Prediction" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>, Steps 6–13

</div>
<div id="alg2.l6" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg2.l6.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">6:</span></span>
              
            <span id="alg2.l6.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">for</span> <math id="alg2.l6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m=\{M,\ldots,1\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m=\{M,\ldots,1\}</annotation></semantics></math> <span id="alg2.l6.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">do</span>

</div>
<div id="alg2.l7" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg2.l7.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">7:</span></span>
              
              Sample <math id="alg2.l7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{y}_{M}\sim\mathcal{N}(\mathbf{0},\bm{I})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐲</mi><mi>M</mi></msub><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>𝟎</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>𝑰</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{y}_{M}\sim\mathcal{N}(\mathbf{0},\bm{I})</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg2.l8" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg2.l8.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">8:</span></span>
              
              Sample <math id="alg2.l8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\epsilon}\sim\mathcal{N}(\mathbf{0},\bm{I})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>𝟎</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>𝑰</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\epsilon}\sim\mathcal{N}(\mathbf{0},\bm{I})</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg2.l9" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg2.l9.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">9:</span></span>
              
              <math id="alg2.l9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{y}_{m-1}=\bm{\mu}_{\theta_{2}}+\frac{\bm{\sigma}_{n}(\mathbf{u})\bm{\sigma}_{m\mid n}(\mathbf{u})}{\bm{\sigma}_{m}(\mathbf{u})}\epsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐲</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝝁</mi><msub><mi>θ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>𝝈</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐮</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝝈</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo fence="false">|</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐮</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝝈</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐮</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{y}_{m-1}=\bm{\mu}_{\theta_{2}}+\frac{\bm{\sigma}_{n}(\mathbf{u})\bm{\sigma}_{m\mid n}(\mathbf{u})}{\bm{\sigma}_{m}(\mathbf{u})}\epsilon</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg2.l10" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg2.l10.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">10:</span></span>
              
            <span id="alg2.l10.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">end</span> <span id="alg2.l10.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">for</span>
</div>
<div id="alg2.l11" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg2.l11.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">11:</span></span>
              
            <math id="alg2.l11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\mathbf{y}}=\mathbf{y}_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐲</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>𝐲</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\mathbf{y}}=\mathbf{y}_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
</div>
</figure>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S5" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">V </span><span id="S5.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Experiments</span></h2>

<section id="S5.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S5.SS1.4" class="ltx_text">V-A</span> </span><span id="S5.SS1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Datasets</span></h3>

<div id="S5.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We evaluate the performance of our proposed model on the ETH/UCY <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">8</a>]</cite> and Stanford Drone Dataset (SDD) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">7</a>]</cite>. The ETH/UCY dataset comprises five distinct scenes (ETH, HOTEL, UNIV, ZARA1, and ZARA2), containing trajectories of 1,536 pedestrians sampled at 2.5 Hz. For ETH/UCY, following prior works <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">10</a>, <a href="#bib.bib57" title="" class="ltx_ref">57</a>, <a href="#bib.bib46" title="" class="ltx_ref">46</a>]</cite>, we adopt the widely used leave-one-scene-out protocol, training the model on four scenes and testing it on the held-out scene. The SDD is a large-scale dataset that captures trajectories of pedestrians, cyclists, and vehicles from a bird’s-eye view on a university campus, providing diverse and complex interaction scenarios for trajectory prediction research.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S5.SS2.4" class="ltx_text">V-B</span> </span><span id="S5.SS2.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Metrics</span></h3>

<div id="S5.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In line with previous research <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">10</a>, <a href="#bib.bib58" title="" class="ltx_ref">58</a>]</cite>, we adopt Average Displacement Error (ADE) and Final Displacement Error (FDE) as evaluation metrics to demonstrate the effectiveness of our proposed method. Specifically, ADE measures the average Euclidean distance between the predicted trajectory and the ground-truth trajectory over all time steps, and is defined as:</p>
<table id="S5.E32" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.E32.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{ADE}=\frac{1}{T_{fut}}\sum_{t=1}^{T_{fut}}\left\|\hat{\mathbf{y}}_{t}-\mathbf{y}_{t}\right\|_{2}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mtext>ADE</mtext><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub></munderover><msub><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐲</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>𝐲</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><mo>‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{ADE}=\frac{1}{T_{fut}}\sum_{t=1}^{T_{fut}}\left\|\hat{\mathbf{y}}_{t}-\mathbf{y}_{t}\right\|_{2},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(32)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.SS2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S5.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\mathbf{y}}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐲</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\mathbf{y}}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{y}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐲</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{y}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> denote the predicted and ground-truth positions at time step <math id="S5.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>, respectively, and <math id="S5.SS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the total length of the trajectory. FDE, on the other hand, measures only the final-step prediction error, and is computed as:</p>
<table id="S5.E33" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.E33.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{FDE}=\left\|\hat{\mathbf{y}}_{T_{fut}}-\mathbf{y}_{T_{fut}}\right\|_{2}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mtext>FDE</mtext><mo>=</mo><msub><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐲</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>𝐲</mi><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub></msub></mrow><mo>‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{FDE}=\left\|\hat{\mathbf{y}}_{T_{fut}}-\mathbf{y}_{T_{fut}}\right\|_{2}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(33)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">To ensure a fair comparison, following prior work <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">10</a>, <a href="#bib.bib46" title="" class="ltx_ref">46</a>, <a href="#bib.bib57" title="" class="ltx_ref">57</a>]</cite>, we generate 20 predicted future trajectories and report the final error as the minimum error across all predictions.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S5.SS3.4" class="ltx_text">V-C</span> </span><span id="S5.SS3.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Baselines</span></h3>

<div id="S5.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To demonstrate the effectiveness of our model, we compare prediction results against both conventional trajectory-prediction baselines and models specifically designed for momentary trajectory prediction:</p>
<ul id="S5.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S5.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S5.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Social GAN</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib59" title="" class="ltx_ref">59</a>]</cite>: Social GAN employs an encoder–decoder architecture with a social-pooling module to capture multi-agent interactions.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S5.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S5.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Next</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib60" title="" class="ltx_ref">60</a>]</cite>: Next leverages an attention-based LSTM coupled with spatial activity localization to extract features from behavioral features and person–scene interactions.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S5.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S5.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.I1.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Social-STGCNN</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib61" title="" class="ltx_ref">61</a>]</cite>: Social-STGCNN proposes a spatio-temporal graph convolutional network that models pedestrians as graph nodes to capture social interactions.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S5.I1.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S5.I1.i4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.I1.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.I1.i4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Trajectron++</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">13</a>]</cite>: Trajectron++ leverages a graph-structured recurrent network to predict the trajectories of a general number of diverse agents.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S5.I1.i5" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S5.I1.i5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.I1.i5.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.I1.i5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">SGCN</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib62" title="" class="ltx_ref">62</a>]</cite>: Pedestrian trajectories are modeled with a sparse graph convolutional approach that uses directed spatial graphs for interactions and directed temporal graphs for motion patterns.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S5.I1.i6" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S5.I1.i6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.I1.i6.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.I1.i6.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">SingularTrajectory</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib63" title="" class="ltx_ref">63</a>]</cite>: A diffusion-based, universal trajectory predictor maps diverse motion tasks into a shared embedding space, enabling generalizable predictions across settings.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S5.I1.i7" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S5.I1.i7.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.I1.i7.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.I1.i7.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">STT+DTO</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">9</a>]</cite>: A knowledge-distillation framework in which a student learns future-trajectory prediction from limited observations by mimicking a teacher trained on full trajectories.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S5.I1.i8" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S5.I1.i8.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.I1.i8.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.I1.i8.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">MID</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib46" title="" class="ltx_ref">46</a>]</cite>: MID proposed a novel transformer-based network to model complex temporal dependency in trajectories.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S5.I1.i9" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S5.I1.i9.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.I1.i9.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.I1.i9.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">EigenTrajectory</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib64" title="" class="ltx_ref">64</a>]</cite>: EigenTrajectory uses low-rank spatio-temporal descriptors to compactly represent and forecast multi-modal pedestrian motion.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S5.I1.i10" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S5.I1.i10.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.I1.i10.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.I1.i10.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">PCCSNet</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib57" title="" class="ltx_ref">57</a>]</cite>: PCCSNet proposes a novel framework that formulates multimodal prediction into three steps.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S5.I1.i11" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S5.I1.i11.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.I1.i11.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.I1.i11.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">SocialVAE</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">16</a>]</cite>: SocialVAE employs a temporally-aware variational autoencoder (VAE) to model the multimodality and uncertainty in human trajectory prediction.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S5.I1.i12" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S5.I1.i12.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.I1.i12.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.I1.i12.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">MOE</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>]</cite>: MOE designs a Momentary Observation feature Extractor to extract the features in momentary observations.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S5.I1.i13" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S5.I1.i13.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.I1.i13.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.I1.i13.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">BCDiff</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">10</a>]</cite>: BCDiff proposes a bidirectional diffusion model that jointly generates unobserved historical and future trajectories.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S5.SS4.4" class="ltx_text">V-D</span> </span><span id="S5.SS4.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Experimental Setup</span></h3>

<div id="S5.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For the forward diffusion process of <math id="S5.SS4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textit{DDPM}_{past}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">DDPM</mtext><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textit{DDPM}_{past}</annotation></semantics></math>, we employ a linear noise schedule, fixing the variance bounds to <math id="S5.SS4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta_{1}=1\times 10^{-4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>β</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta_{1}=1\times 10^{-4}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.SS4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta_{M}=0.05" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>β</mi><mi>M</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0.05</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta_{M}=0.05</annotation></semantics></math>. Our encoder uses exactly the hyper-parameter configuration reported in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>]</cite>. Separately, the denoising backbone is composed of three Transformer encoder blocks, each with a model dimension of 128, a 256-dimensional feed-forward sub-layer, and 4 self-attention heads.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS4.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS4.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS4.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Diffusion<sup id="S5.SS4.p2.1.1.1" class="ltx_sup">2</sup></span> was implemented in PyTorch and trained on a server running Ubuntu 22.04, equipped with an Intel Xeon Platinum 8358P (2.60 GHz) CPU and a single NVIDIA A800-SXM4-80G GPU. Training was carried out for 100 epochs with a batch size of 256.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS5" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S5.SS5.4" class="ltx_text">V-E</span> </span><span id="S5.SS5.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Main Results</span></h3>

<div id="S5.SS5.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS5.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Comparison with State-of-the-Art Methods.</span></p>
</div>
<figure id="S5.T1" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">TABLE I: </span>Comparisons of various approaches on multiple datasets. The evaluation results are reported as ADE/FDE (m). The best result is highlighted in bold, and the runner-up is <span id="S5.T1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_underline">underlined</span>.</figcaption>
<div id="S5.T1.3" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_center ltx_transformed_outer" style="width:345.0pt;height:110.9pt;vertical-align:-54.1pt;"><span class="ltx_transformed_inner" style="transform:translate(-148.5pt,47.7pt) scale(0.537430017676305,0.537430017676305) ;">
