</span></div>
</figure>
<div id="S5.SS5.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS5.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We validate our proposed method on multiple datasets and the overall performance is shown in Tab. <a href="#S5.T1" title="TABLE I ‣ V-E Main Results ‣ V Experiments ‣ Diffusion2: Dual Diffusion Model with Uncertainty-Aware Adaptive Noise for Momentary Trajectory Prediction" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">I</span></a>. The experimental results clearly show that <span id="S5.SS5.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Diffusion<sup id="S5.SS5.p2.1.1.1" class="ltx_sup">2</sup></span> outperforms the baseline models on most datasets. Specifically, the ADE and FDE of <span id="S5.SS5.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Diffusion<sup id="S5.SS5.p2.1.2.1" class="ltx_sup">2</sup></span> are markedly lower than the current state-of-the-art (SOTA) results. For example, on HOTEL, our ADE (0.10) is 23.1% lower than the SOTA value (0.13), and our FDE (0.13) is 35.0% lower than the SOTA (0.20). On ETH, our FDE (0.45) shows a 19.6% reduction compared to the SOTA (0.56). Averaged across ETH/UCY, we obtain an FDE of 0.33, which is 15.4% below the SOTA value of 0.39. Our ADE is 0.19, matching the reported SOTA (0.19) and exhibiting a slight improvement on the order of <math id="S5.SS5.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="10^{-3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">10^{-3}</annotation></semantics></math>.
On SDD, <span id="S5.SS5.p2.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Diffusion<sup id="S5.SS5.p2.1.3.1" class="ltx_sup">2</sup></span> achieves an ADE/FDE of 8.26/14.87. This sets a new best ADE, improving upon the prior lowest value (8.32) by about 0.7%, and the FDE essentially ties the best reported number (14.86), with a difference of 0.01 (i.e., <math id="S5.SS5.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="&lt;10^{-2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>&lt;</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">&lt;10^{-2}</annotation></semantics></math>). These results suggest that our uncertainty-aware dual-head parameterization and adaptive noise scheduling transfer well to aerial-view, heterogeneous multi-agent scenes.
Compared with previous methods, <span id="S5.SS5.p2.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Diffusion<sup id="S5.SS5.p2.1.4.1" class="ltx_sup">2</sup></span> first uses a dual-head parameterization mechanism to estimate the uncertainty of historical trajectory predictions. Then, the adaptive noise scheduling module dynamically adjusts the forward noise addition process according to the uncertainty. Through these modules, the framework encourages the extraction and learning of informative features, thereby improving trajectory generation quality. Although overall performance is competitive, our method trails the state of the art on datasets such as UNIV. A plausible explanation is that the UNIV scene contains richer, more intricate social interactions (e.g., higher crowd density and frequent group formations), as shown in Fig. <a href="#S5.F5" title="Fig. 5 ‣ V-F Qualitative evaluation. ‣ V Experiments ‣ Diffusion2: Dual Diffusion Model with Uncertainty-Aware Adaptive Noise for Momentary Trajectory Prediction" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>. These cases motivate further refinement of our framework for better adaptability to diverse scene dynamics.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS5.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS5.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS5.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Learned Adaptive Noise Schedule.</span></p>
</div>
<figure id="S5.F3" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2510.04365v1/sigma.png" id="S5.F3.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/167;" width="476" height="167" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Fig. 3: </span>A visualization of the learned <math id="S5.F3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.F3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="l" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>l</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">l</annotation></semantics></math> over diffusion step <math id="S5.F3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math>, where each <math id="S5.F3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="l_{m}=\operatorname{sigmoid}(\gamma_{\varphi}(\mathbf{u},m))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>l</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>sigmoid</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>γ</mi><mi>φ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐮</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">l_{m}=\operatorname{sigmoid}(\gamma_{\varphi}(\mathbf{u},m))</annotation></semantics></math>.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S5.SS5.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS5.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Fig. <a href="#S5.F3" title="Fig. 3 ‣ V-E Main Results ‣ V Experiments ‣ Diffusion2: Dual Diffusion Model with Uncertainty-Aware Adaptive Noise for Momentary Trajectory Prediction" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> presents the learned schedules of the averaged <math id="S5.SS5.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{m}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.SS5.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="l_{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>l</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">l_{m}</annotation></semantics></math> over the diffusion steps <math id="S5.SS5.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math>. The colour gradient—from blue (low predictive uncertainty) to red (high predictive uncertainty)—highlights how the model adapts its noise‐injection strategy to the input’s uncertainty level. For low‐uncertainty inputs (blue curves), <math id="S5.SS5.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="l_{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>l</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">l_{m}</annotation></semantics></math> rises steeply, so stronger noise is injected early in the forward process. Conversely, high‐uncertainty inputs (red curves) show a slower increase, delaying and attenuating the injected noise. The underlying reason for this attenuation is that highly uncertain inputs must preserve fragile signal features; by adding less noise, the model avoids drowning these features and enables the reverse process to recover them more reliably during reconstruction.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS5.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS5.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS5.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Inference Latency.</span>
In our proposed <span id="S5.SS5.p5.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Diffusion<sup id="S5.SS5.p5.1.2.1" class="ltx_sup">2</sup></span> framework, the DDPM-based sampler is a plug-and-play module that can be replaced by more efficient diffusion samplers without changing the rest of the architecture. We benchmark the inference latency of representative baselines and our model (with both DDPM and DDIM sampling) on identical hardware (NVIDIA RTX A800). As summarized in Table <a href="#S5.T2" title="TABLE II ‣ V-E Main Results ‣ V Experiments ‣ Diffusion2: Dual Diffusion Model with Uncertainty-Aware Adaptive Noise for Momentary Trajectory Prediction" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">II</span></a>, our DDPM-based instantiation runs at <math id="S5.SS5.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="412" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>412</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">412</annotation></semantics></math> ms, which is slower than discriminative baselines MID (<math id="S5.SS5.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="325" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>325</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">325</annotation></semantics></math> ms) and PCCSNet (<math id="S5.SS5.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="107" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>107</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">107</annotation></semantics></math> ms). This gap mainly comes from the multi-step Markovian denoising inherent to DDPM. Replacing the sampler with DDIM collapses the sampling chain to far fewer steps, reducing latency to <math id="S5.SS5.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="75" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>75</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">75</annotation></semantics></math> ms without changing the architecture, thus meeting real-time requirements. BCDiff <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">10</a>]</cite>, while also reconstructing unobserved history and predicting future trajectories like ours, is slow due to its iterative bidirectional refinement in the BCDU module (about <math id="S5.SS5.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M{=}40" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>40</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M{=}40</annotation></semantics></math> steps), which incurs many forward passes and yields high latency (<math id="S5.SS5.p5.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sim" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>∼</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sim</annotation></semantics></math>4 s). Meanwhile, many recent methods <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib48" title="" class="ltx_ref">48</a>, <a href="#bib.bib65" title="" class="ltx_ref">65</a>, <a href="#bib.bib66" title="" class="ltx_ref">66</a>]</cite> have been proposed to accelerate inference by loosening Markovian constraints and generating trajectories with fewer or skipped steps, offering substantial potential to further reduce the inference latency of <span id="S5.SS5.p5.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Diffusion<sup id="S5.SS5.p5.1.3.1" class="ltx_sup">2</sup></span>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS5.p6" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS5.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS5.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Reverse Diffusion Process.</span>
As Fig. <a href="#S5.F4" title="Fig. 4 ‣ V-E Main Results ‣ V Experiments ‣ Diffusion2: Dual Diffusion Model with Uncertainty-Aware Adaptive Noise for Momentary Trajectory Prediction" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a> illustrates, we visualize the denoising dynamics of the <math id="S5.SS5.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textit{DDPM}_{fut}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">DDPM</mtext><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textit{DDPM}_{fut}</annotation></semantics></math>, rendering snapshots every 20 inference steps <math id="S5.SS5.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(m\in\{0,20,\dots,200\})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>20</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>200</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(m\in\{0,20,\dots,200\})</annotation></semantics></math>. Starting from an initial Gaussian noise at <math id="S5.SS5.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m=0</annotation></semantics></math>, the reverse process progressively suppresses uncertainty and converges toward a coherent future trajectory consistent with the observed history. Owing to the minimal distributional divergence observed during the initial 160 steps, intermediate samples for <math id="S5.SS5.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m\in\{60,\dots,120\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>60</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>120</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m\in\{60,\dots,120\}</annotation></semantics></math> are omitted to emphasize the salient transitions.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S5.F4" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2510.04365v1/denoising_process.png" id="S5.F4.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:429/207;" width="429" height="207" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Fig. 4: </span>Visualization of generated trajectories across reverse-diffusion time <math id="S5.F4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math>. The red and green lines represent ground-truth future and observable trajectories. As <math id="S5.F4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math> increases, the process progressively suppresses indeterminacy and converges toward the desired future path. Initialized from a pure gaussian noise at <math id="S5.F4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m=0</annotation></semantics></math> and conditioned on context information, the model gradually removes implausible hypotheses and aligns with the ground-truth future (red, solid).
</figcaption>
</figure>
<figure id="S5.T2" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering" style="font-size:90%;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">TABLE II: </span>Inference latency comparison on identical hardware (NVIDIA RTX A800).</figcaption>
<table id="S5.T2.3" class="ltx_tabular ltx_centering ltx_align_middle">
<tr id="S5.T2.3.1" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S5.T2.3.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_tt" style="padding-left:8.0pt;padding-right:8.0pt;"><span id="S5.T2.3.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:90%;">Method</span></td>
<td id="S5.T2.3.1.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_border_tt" style="padding-left:8.0pt;padding-right:8.0pt;"><span id="S5.T2.3.1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:90%;">Latency (ms)</span></td></tr>
<tr id="S5.T2.3.2" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S5.T2.3.2.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_t" style="padding-left:8.0pt;padding-right:8.0pt;"><span id="S5.T2.3.2.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">MID <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib46" title="" class="ltx_ref">46</a>]</cite></span></td>
